WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.653
- Welcome, everyone.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:11.250
This is the second of a
two-part lecture series,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.683
the 2023 Castle Lecture series.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.700
For those who were not here yesterday,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.570
I am pleased to introduce our
speaker today, Ben Wizner,

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.680
who is the director

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.570
of the ACLU Speech Privacy
and Technology Project.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.700
Yesterday, Ben started to build up

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.410
the foundations of his argument

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.280
and deliver an engaging and
thought-provoking account

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.490
of a Freedom of Information Act lawsuit

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.370
seeking to release a few
dozen pages of records

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.710
from the Guantanamo Bay prison.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.330
This lawsuit, as he argued,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.030
illustrates how the national security law

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.730
affects democratic accountability.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.820
Today, Ben will continue
his critical analysis

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.160
of the national security law.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.750
And as I mentioned yesterday,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.800
the Castle Lectures aim
to promote awareness

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.910
of the ethical challenges and
complexities of our society,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.300
and foster a robust dialogue
of the moral foundations

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.310
of society and government.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.710
This lecture series were made possible

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.140
because of a generous gift by John Castle,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.363
who is in the audience with us today.

00:01:19.311 --> 00:01:20.144
(audience applauding)

00:01:20.144 --> 00:01:20.977
Thank you so much.

00:01:22.104 --> 00:01:25.710
We are grateful for the
support of Mr. Castle,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.850
both to the EPME program,
and for Yale more broadly.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.760
This gift was made in
honor of his ancestor,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.710
Rev. James Pierpont,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.810
a Harvard-trained congressional minister

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.490
who founded the Collegiate
School of Connecticut in 1701,

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.903
soon thereafter renamed for
its benefactor, Elihu Yale.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.340
We are very grateful to Ben
for accepting the invitation

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.770
to deliver this Yale lecture,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.710
and for his willingness to
discuss with our community

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.350
such a relevant and complex topic

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.200
as the interconnection
of national security

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.630
and democratic accountability.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.280
The format of today's lecture

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.620
is going to be the same as yesterday.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.890
So Ben will talk for about 50 minutes,

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.890
and then we're gonna have
plenty of time for Q&amp;A.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.720
So I please ask you to welcome
me in giving Ben the floor.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.377
Ben, thank you so much
for being with us today.

00:02:22.377 --> 00:02:25.470
(audience applauding)

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:26.460
- Thank you very much, Anna,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.710
for that kind introduction again,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.470
and thank you all for being
here, especially those

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.753
who I'm seeing for the
second day in a row.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.250
If anything, to those of you who are new,

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.300
seems underdeveloped,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.766
it's simply because you
weren't here yesterday.

00:02:41.766 --> 00:02:45.450
(audience laughing)

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.240
In September of 2011,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.400
Harvard Law School and
the Brookings Institution,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.140
which publishes the "Lawfare" blog,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.890
a kind of online clubhouse
for the National Security set,

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.750
hosted a two-day conference

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.380
to mark the 10 year anniversary
of the 9/11 attacks.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.380
Among the event's
luminaries was John Brennan,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.990
at that time, Obama's chief
counterterrorism advisor,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.960
and later the director
of Central Intelligence,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.720
who delivered a lunchtime keynote address.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.330
I joined the queue of people
waiting to ask him a question,

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.970
and when my turn came I asked,

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.893
does the CIA have a drone program?

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.730
The audience erupted in laughter.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.210
Brennan responded, "Well,
as you know, covert actions

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:29.857
are designed to be covert and therefore."

00:03:29.857 --> 00:03:31.920
"And therefore you only read
about them in the newspaper,"

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.763
I interrupted.

00:03:33.997 --> 00:03:36.390
"If the agency did have such a program,"

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.017
he said to more laughter,

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:39.960
"I'm sure it would be done

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.300
with the utmost care and precision

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.340
in accordance with the law and our values

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.437
if such a program existed."

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.710
At the close of yesterday's lecture,

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.170
we discussed the legal
fiction that forms the pillar

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:55.410
of the intelligence
community's impunity regime,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.850
that there is a critical distinction

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.040
between what is universally known,

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.620
and what is officially confirmed.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.510
Anyone unfamiliar with that fiction

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.780
would've been bewildered by
my exchange with Brennan.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.580
Wasn't the CIA's drone program

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.250
in newspapers almost every day?

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.090
And weren't those stories

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.523
essentially readouts
from government sources?

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:20.100
Two weeks after the Harvard
Conference, Anwar al-Awlaki,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.190
a US citizen born in
Las Cruces, New Mexico,

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.860
was killed in Yemen by a hellfire missile

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.350
fired from a US predator drone.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.890
This targeted killing came as no surprise.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.480
The entire world knew

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.000
that the United States had
marked al-Awlaki for death

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.070
nearly two years earlier.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.930
That's because in early 2010,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.790
senior Obama administration officials,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.160
in a coordinated media strategy,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.680
leaked to leading
newspapers that al-Awlaki

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.350
had been added to secret kill lists

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.930
maintained by the CIA and
Department of Defense's

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.510
Joint Special Operations Command.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.790
These off-the-record leaks

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.250
were a deliberate policy decision.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.500
The Obama administration wanted the public

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.140
to know of its controversial decision

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.000
to target a US citizen for death

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.273
based on undisclosed
criteria and evidence.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.240
In national security law,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.820
we always arrive, sooner or later,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.583
at the bottom of a previously
announced slippery slope.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.120
At oral argument before the
Fourth Circuit Court of Appeals

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.730
in the al-Masri case, which
I discussed yesterday,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.650
I had warned the court

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.960
that if al-Masri's claims
of kidnapping and torture

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.330
could be dismissed on secrecy grounds,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.130
then the state secrets privilege

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.740
will be employed routinely
to shield from liability

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.480
even the most egregious acts.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.230
The government could and would
make the identical argument,

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:43.200
even if the claim involved
extrajudicial murder,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.110
and even if the plaintiff
were an American citizen.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:48.123
Could and would and did.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.597
In August of 2010,

00:05:51.597 --> 00:05:54.840
the ACLU and the Center
for Constitutional Rights

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.450
responded to the slew of
news stories announcing

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.600
that al-Awlaki had been added
to government kill lists

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.100
by filing a lawsuit,

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.170
challenging the government's
asserted authority

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.660
to carry out targeted
killings of US citizens

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.020
located far from any armed conflict zone.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.580
The US was not at war with
Yemen or within it, we argued,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.930
and outside of armed conflict,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.450
both the Constitution
and international law

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.720
prohibited deliberate killing,
except as a last resort

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.310
to protect against concrete,
specific, and imminent threats.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.200
An extrajudicial killing policy

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.840
under which names were
added to kill lists,

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.880
shopping lists for death,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.550
through some kind of
secret executive process,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.200
and remain there for months,

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.770
was plainly not limited
to imminent threats.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.050
And because al-Awlaki was a US citizen,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.280
he had a due process right

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.490
to know what conduct might
subject him to execution

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.890
at the hands of his own government.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.680
In response to our
lawsuit, the government,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.140
having deliberately
invited a public debate

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.230
about its asserted
authority to kill al-Awlaki,

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.020
now decided that having
that debate in a courtroom

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.500
would reveal state secrets
and damage national security.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.190
In a sworn declaration,
Secretary of Defense Robert Gates

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:11.040
insisted that quote,
"disclosure of whether or not

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.510
lethal force has been authorized

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.700
to combat a terrorist
organization overseas,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.610
and if so, the specific
targets of such action,

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.590
would provide the nation's enemies

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.910
with critical information
needed to evade hostile action."

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.890
This was head spinning.

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:27.300
If it was true,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.180
it was quite obviously
the administration's leaks

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.170
and not our lawsuit that
had created that danger.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:37.863
This pattern, placing information
into the public sphere,

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.730
and then claiming secrecy when
faced with legal challenge

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.290
is an all too common feature

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.720
of national security law in practice.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.610
In February of 2008,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.130
I was in a federal court
in San Jose, California

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.770
arguing against the dismissal of a lawsuit

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:53.910
brought by five victims

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.850
of the CIA's rendition
and torture program.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.800
The basis for the
government's motion to dismiss

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.940
was a sworn declaration by
CIA director Michael Hayden

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.070
insisting that any confirmation or denial

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.660
of the lawsuit's allegations

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.780
would disclose state secrets
and damage national security.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:11.730
On that very same day,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.550
Hayden himself was
testifying before Congress,

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.010
and disclosing officially
for the first time

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:20.400
that the CIA had
waterboarded three prisoners.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.680
I should add, just two months before that,

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.830
Hayden had written in a
letter to CIA employees

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:28.860
that the CIA had destroyed 92 videotapes

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.870
depicting enhanced
interrogation techniques

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.910
because the CIA had determined

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:34.887
that they were not relevant

00:08:34.887 --> 00:08:37.350
to any congressional
or judicial proceeding.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.493
This is the expertise to
which courts obligingly defer.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.630
The government's legal
fictions around secrecy

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.570
can produce some amusing results.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.350
In November of 2010, WikiLeaks
and its media partners

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:53.940
began publishing a quarter of a million

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.640
confidential State Department cables

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:00.270
that they had obtained from
Army private Chelsea Manning.

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:01.170
Hundreds of thousands

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.510
of government employees and contractors

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.310
were immediately warned by their superiors

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.110
not to view the cables.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.880
Even those published in full

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.920
by "The New York Times"
and other media outlets

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.340
because they might lack the
proper clearance to do so.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.800
I was under no such injunction,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.080
and I found some pretty interesting cables

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:20.610
describing the diplomatic fallout

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.680
from the CIA's rendition
and torture programs,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.130
from Guantanamo, and from drone strikes.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:30.219
In 2011, the ACLU submitted a FOIA request

00:09:30.219 --> 00:09:32.910
to the State Department
for 23 of those cables.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:35.580
There was an element of
mischief in this request.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.320
It was the first and only time

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.240
we had used the FOIA to ask for documents

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.330
that we literally
already held in our hand,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:46.320
but we were curious, what
would the State Department do?

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.800
Would they continue to
withhold the cables in full?

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.220
Would they bow to reality
and declassify them,

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.630
or would they process the request

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.640
by redacting what they deemed sensitive

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.710
and releasing the rest?

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:01.860
If you chose option C, hats off to you.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.830
Sure enough, the State Department
sent us redacted versions

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:05.970
of 11 of the cables

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.690
offering a rare roadmap of
their classification process.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:12.480
The ACLU created a web
feature that allowed readers

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.730
to hover the mouse over
the redacted portions

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.640
to see exactly what had been removed.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.390
This included a reference to allegations

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:22.620
of Italian prosecutors

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.710
that a US military jet had
traversed Swiss airspace

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:26.820
on the day that the CIA

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.710
kidnapped cleric Abu Omar from
Milan, that was no secret.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.390
Two years earlier an Italian court

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:35.460
had convicted in absentia 23 Americans,

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:36.840
most of them CIA operatives,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.720
for their role in his abduction
and rendition to Egypt

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.370
where he was tortured.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:42.570
Also redacted was a cable

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:45.300
describing the pressure the
US had placed on Germany

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.920
to shut down a criminal investigation

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.830
into the CIA's kidnapping
of Khalid al-Masri

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.860
and another explaining how
the Abu Ghraib torture scandal

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.173
had made the US less secure.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.010
I called this result amusing,

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:00.300
but in secrecy disputes,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.090
the government gets the last laugh.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:06.300
We argued in court that by
processing our FOIA request,

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:07.470
the government had acknowledged

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:09.810
that the cables were authentic.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.790
That meant, or should have meant,

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:14.160
that the State Department
could no longer hang its hat

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.470
on the distinction between what was known

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.290
and what was confirmed,
and should now be compelled

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.990
to declassify the cables in full.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.930
But a federal court sided
with the government.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:25.950
because our FOIA request

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.950
had listed the cables by date and number,

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:31.410
and had not specifically
mentioned WikiLeaks,

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.600
the State Department had
actually admitted nothing

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:34.980
by processing our request,

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.010
they might be different cables together.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:38.843
Of course,

00:11:38.843 --> 00:11:41.040
the court could reach this
embarrassing conclusion

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.470
only by refusing to take 30 seconds

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.510
to look at the cables
published by WikiLeaks

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.303
and those held by the
government side by side.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.860
John Brennan's cheeky
refusal to acknowledge

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:56.130
the CIA's drone program
was a hit at Harvard.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.670
The academic audience
appreciated being in on the joke,

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.710
but the game the government
plays in these cases

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.170
is corrosive and damaging.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.810
By deploying selective leaks to the press,

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:09.180
and secrecy claims to the courts,

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.600
the government can simultaneously
manipulate public debate

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.510
and avoid accountability and oversight.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:18.390
And of course the flip side
of impunity for insiders

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:21.330
is strict liability for dissenters.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.240
The Espionage Act of 1917,
the principle vehicle

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.300
for prosecuting national security leaks,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.880
does not distinguish between
unauthorized leaks to the press

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:30.990
in the public interest,

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:34.140
and the sale of secrets
to foreign enemies,

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.790
which is to say it doesn't
distinguish between

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:38.580
sources and spies.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:40.380
In fact, in a recent leak prosecution,

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.170
the Department of Justice
took the remarkable position

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:45.060
that unauthorized leaks to the press

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:48.390
were more pernicious than
the typical espionage case

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:51.420
where a spy sells classified
information for money,

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.070
because when information
appears in the press,

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:57.663
every foreign adversary, and
not just one, stood to benefit.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.330
All too often national security elites

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.970
regard the American public
as their adversaries,

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.230
and place their own interests

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.813
ahead of the values of an open society.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.700
It's understandable that
in the immediate aftermath

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:14.160
of a national trauma like 9/11,

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.990
courts would give the executive branch

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:19.260
a longer leash on matters
of national security.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:20.880
What's harder to explain

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:22.680
is how this excessive judicial deference

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.020
amounting to de facto impunity

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:28.383
has endured and entrenched
itself over time.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.240
In a speech at Hebrew
University in Jerusalem in 1987,

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.060
the late justice William Brennan lamented

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.370
the shabby treatment civil
liberties have received

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.530
in the United States in times of war,

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:43.440
or perceived threats to national security.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.840
He described a cyclical pattern.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.240
In an atmosphere of crisis,
courts would be in his words,

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.280
reluctant to question the factual bases

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.740
underlying asserted security threats,

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:54.330
and would defer to executive demands

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.450
that liberties be temporarily curtailed.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:13:59.490
After each perceived
security crisis ended,

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:03.000
Brennan observed the United
States has remorsefully realized

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.360
that the abrogation of civil
liberties was unnecessary,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.663
and the claimed dangers
wildly exaggerated.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.650
Quote, "The rumors of French
intrigue during the 1790s,

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.320
the claims that civilian courts
were unable to adjudicate

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.390
the allegedly treasonous
actions of northerners

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:19.800
during the Civil War.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:21.240
The hysterical belief

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.550
that criticism of conscription
and the war effort

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.680
might lead droves of
soldiers to desert the army

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:27.810
or resist the draft during World War I,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.540
the wild assertions of
sabotage or espionage

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.540
by Japanese Americans during World War II,

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.970
and the paranoid fear that
the American Communist Party

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:37.770
stood ready to overthrow the government,

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.860
were all so baseless that
they would be comical

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.140
were it not for the serious
hardship that they caused

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:45.240
during the time of crisis," Brennan noted.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.000
Nevertheless, the country had proved

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.410
unable to break this cycle

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:53.460
because Brennan believed the
crises were too infrequent

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:55.380
and the country too tranquil

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.090
for courts to develop the expertise

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.910
to scrutinize claimed threats.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:01.530
Quote, "A jurisprudence capable

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.080
of braving the overblown
claims of national security

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.300
must be forged in times of crisis

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:07.920
by the sort of intimate familiarity

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.810
with national security threats

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.120
that tests their bases in fact,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.750
explores their relation to the
exercise of civil freedoms,

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.290
and probes the limits of
their compass," he insisted.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.000
In this way he hoped adversity

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.463
could be the handmaiden of liberty.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.120
If Justice Brennan had been correct,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.520
if courts simply needed more exposure

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:30.840
to national security claims

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:34.170
to develop the institutional
capability to assess them,

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:36.670
then the last two decades
should have provided it.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:38.520
But it now seems clear

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:41.760
that Justice Brennan's
optimism was misplaced.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.010
Have courts learned to
test the factual bases

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:46.140
of claimed national security threats?

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.520
To the contrary,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.440
they've doubled down on
a doctrine of deference

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:52.260
that insist on their own
institutional incompetence

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:54.660
to second guess security officials.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.060
Have courts explored the
relation of asserted threats

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.340
to the exercise of civil freedoms?

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:00.173
To the contrary,

00:16:00.173 --> 00:16:02.970
they've treated national
security interests as absolute,

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.310
and resisted a jurisprudence
of proportionality

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:09.990
that would contextualize threats
and weigh competing values.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:11.940
Have courts probed the
limits of the compass

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.310
of the government's claims of crisis?

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:15.210
To the contrary,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.400
they've acquiesced to
a definition of crisis

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.280
wholly lacking in temporal
or geographic constraints,

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:24.543
a conflict that takes place
everywhere and forever.

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.770
Justice Brennan assumed
that national crises

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.830
would be defined or at least definable.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:33.600
He had in mind wars or conflicts

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.000
of fixed duration and scope.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.580
The post-9/11 National Security Project,

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.370
with its focus on countering
terrorist threats,

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:42.780
has in the words of one scholar

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.030
identified the perfect conceptual vehicle

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:48.960
for a permanent mobilization
of the security state.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.440
And national security law in
the age of counterterrorism

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:56.070
has made judicial abdication
not the cyclical exception

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:57.840
that Justice Brennan described,

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:02.250
but an entrenched norm that
John Brennan no doubt prefers.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.860
That courts have embraced
this diminished role,

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.230
and if anything, deepened
their commitment to deference

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.780
despite the absence of
a major terrorist attack

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:12.120
on US soil since 9/11,

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:15.510
is a testament to the success
of national security elites

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:19.233
in enshrining into law a
permanent state of exception.

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.350
What is national security?

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:25.410
The term is overused and under-analyzed.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.840
And it's so rotely intoned
in so many contexts

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:31.380
that it's been drained
of almost all meaning.

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:34.200
In his memoir, Graham Greene
tells the story of a friend

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:35.250
who, at her father's funeral,

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:38.827
is urged by an elderly priest
to return to the church.

00:17:38.827 --> 00:17:40.837
"Remind me," she asked the priest,

00:17:40.837 --> 00:17:43.867
"what are the arguments
for the existence of God?"

00:17:43.867 --> 00:17:45.907
"I knew them once," replies the priest,

00:17:45.907 --> 00:17:47.207
"but I've forgotten them."

00:17:48.573 --> 00:17:49.620
(audience laughs)

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:52.260
There's something similarly ritualistic

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.240
in the way we invoke national security

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:56.880
without attending to
the meaning of the words

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:00.000
or to what they do or don't encompass.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.040
Victor Klemperer, in
a much darker context,

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.470
described how words, idioms,
and sentence structures

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:07.320
can be imposed on a people
in a million repetitions,

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:10.920
and taken on board
mechanically and unconsciously.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:12.030
In Klemperer's understanding,

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:15.780
the process is a closed system
for undermining meaning.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:17.610
By forcing a public debate using words

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:20.010
and even categories of its own invention,

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:22.350
a government can manufacture consent

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:24.390
through the manufacture of language.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:25.890
The press, merely by doing its job,

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.630
amplifies the new terminology.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:29.160
Even if it does so critically,

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:32.160
it writes the concept into being.

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.470
National security has entered
our collective bloodstream

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:35.940
through these endless repetitions

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.360
and has enshrined itself as
a defining national interest

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.760
without being clearly defined.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:42.630
I don't mean to suggest

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:46.350
that the law provides no
definitions, there are many,

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:49.110
but over time, the definitional
scope of national security

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:52.200
has been continually revised and expanded

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:54.450
to include other
recapitulated terminology,

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.920
like Homeland Security
or counter-terrorism.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:00.420
National security is now understood

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:03.990
to encompass national defense
functions on one side,

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:07.650
and domestic law enforcement
functions on the other.

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:09.930
With few, if any, limiting principles.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:13.240
And because national
security law came into being

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:16.200
in the Cold War context of an
existential nuclear threat,

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.090
once we place the national security label

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:19.470
on a government function,

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:23.370
a distinct set of rules and
expectations comes with it.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:26.070
For example, we've long understood

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.200
that in our constitutional system,

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.810
even murder suspects must walk free

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:34.560
if police or prosecutors break the rules.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.200
But when the context is national security,

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.690
our risk tolerance evaporates,

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:41.610
and we conjure a new category of people

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:43.440
who cannot be released,

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:45.750
even to enforce fundamental rights

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:48.210
or vindicate core values.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.100
The Guantanamo prison remains open

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:51.990
in part because during the brief window

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:54.690
when the Obama administration
could have emptied it,

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:56.940
its own interagency task force concluded

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:58.830
that 48 of the prisoners

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:02.137
could neither be prosecuted nor released.

00:20:02.137 --> 00:20:03.450
"They couldn't be prosecuted,"

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.287
the task force candidly stated,

00:20:05.287 --> 00:20:06.900
"either because there was insufficient

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:08.700
admissible evidence of guilt,"

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.400
meaning evidence that had
not been beaten out of them,

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.580
or "because their conduct
did not constitute

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:17.823
a chargeable offense," meaning
they had committed no crime.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.270
And they couldn't be released,
in the task force's words,

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.310
because "they posed a
national security threat

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.620
that cannot be sufficiently mitigated

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:29.160
through feasible and
appropriate security measures."

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.080
What does that sentence mean?

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:33.540
My question is sincere, not rhetorical.

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:35.940
Cannot be released in what sense?

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:37.950
Why do we casually accept the assertion

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:41.160
that the release of any
individual, any 50 individuals,

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:42.660
poses a national security threat

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.850
that requires indefinite detention

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:48.030
by the most powerful
military in human history?

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.860
What kind of threat?

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:51.900
Is our global surveillance apparatus

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.780
incapable of keeping an
eye on a few dozen people

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:57.690
who have been in our custody
for a decade, now two?

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:00.060
Does the risk that some
might rejoin the Taliban

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:03.270
or even Al-Qaeda, create so great a danger

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:05.070
that we have no reasonable alternative

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:06.753
to locking them away forever?

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:09.060
In short, why isn't a statement like this

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:12.123
greeted with incredulity, even mockery?

00:21:13.356 --> 00:21:16.020
To be sure, the decision to continue

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.880
the Guantanamo indefinite detention regime

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:19.800
has drawn considerable criticism,

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.630
including from the legal academy.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.640
That criticism has rightly taken issue

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:25.920
with the legal basis
for prolonged detention,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.370
and has challenged the assertion
that the military response

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:32.220
authorized by Congress
in the aftermath of 9/11

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.950
includes the authority
to hold people forever,

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:38.493
or until terrorism has been
defeated, whichever comes first.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:40.948
You can laugh.

00:21:40.948 --> 00:21:41.820
(audience laughs)

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:45.300
But we rarely hear criticism
of the childish premise

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.760
that terror suspects are supervillains

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:50.130
who will scatter death
like dandelion seeds

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:52.020
if sprung from their cages.

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.540
You're far more likely to
hear Guantanamo opponents

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.270
insist that in no conceivable scenario

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:00.420
will Abu Zubayda be allowed to
walk the streets of Chicago.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:02.640
If he can't be convicted
in a federal court,

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.410
he'll be put in immigration detention

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:06.450
and deported immediately.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.640
This motive argument has
surely been strategic.

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:10.290
I've used it myself,

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.290
but I've come to regard it as misguided.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.080
By not objecting to the
hyperbolic threat inflation

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:19.170
that's deployed to
justify lawless actions,

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:22.650
by not insisting on a taxonomy
of national security threats

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:26.610
that distinguishes the
existential from the routine,

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.710
we drastically narrow the range of options

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:31.920
that are deemed reasonable
by the public policy makers

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.680
and ultimately the courts.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:37.140
We get trapped in that
claustrophobic fallacy

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:41.100
familiar to anyone who
opposed the invasion of Iraq

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.990
20 years ago this month,

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:44.160
but what would you do

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.413
about Saddam's weapons
of mass destruction?

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:51.660
A few years ago I pulled
into an upstate rest area

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:55.710
on the New York state throughway
on a quiet summer afternoon

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.473
and picked up a brochure
from an information kiosk.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.320
Published by a state
law enforcement agency,

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:05.040
it was headlined, know the
eight signs of terrorism,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:07.500
and it urged the public to be vigilant

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.660
for telltale indicators.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:10.770
Be on the lookout for people

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:14.250
who are taking pictures
or making drawings.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:16.890
Watch for people who are in
places they should not be,

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.000
and people who don't fit

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.670
into the daily routine of your community.

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:23.670
Be especially on guard for the dry run.

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:25.890
Described as putting people into position

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:27.240
and moving them around

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.703
without actually committing
a terrorist attack.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:31.890
A quick internet search

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.020
reveals that versions of this document,

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:36.210
our generation's duck and cover,

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.830
have been republished and distributed

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:41.220
by law enforcement agencies
across the country.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:44.220
Americans everywhere, it
would seem, are being enlisted

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:47.403
to conjure dark fantasies
from routine observations.

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:50.580
My sense is that documents like these

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:54.000
are aimed not only at the
public, but at the police,

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.770
the state and local law
enforcement agencies

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:57.990
that long to be part of the action.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:00.810
The cops on the beat who
are invited to identify

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:03.330
as warriors in the great struggle.

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:05.850
Not only in Manhattan and Los Angeles,

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:08.220
but in police departments nationwide,

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:11.160
suburban and rural officers
are sitting in training rooms

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:14.820
looking at slide decks that
depict ordinary American scenes

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:15.870
and being initiated

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:18.270
into the apparitional
world of counterterrorism.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:22.050
Another kind of threat conjuring
took place in Hollywood

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:24.210
soon after the 9/11 attacks,

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.160
as described breathlessly

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:29.640
in the Hollywood Insider
publication, "The Wrap,"

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:33.480
a lot of what transpired that
night is still top secret,

00:24:33.480 --> 00:24:34.730
but this much we do know.

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:37.470
One evening in October, 2001,

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.090
just weeks after Al-Qaeda terrorists

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:40.650
took down the Twin Towers in Manhattan

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.030
and maimed the Pentagon,

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.180
a group of about 30 of
Hollywood's top creatives,

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:47.317
A-listers like Oliver Stone,

00:24:47.317 --> 00:24:48.997
"Law and Order" producer Dick Wolf,

00:24:48.997 --> 00:24:50.437
"Seven" director David Fincher,

00:24:50.437 --> 00:24:53.190
"NYPD Blues'" David Milch,
and the late John Singleton,

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:56.040
to name just a few, were quietly invited

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.250
to mingle with high-level
members of the US military.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:01.650
The group was officially, though secretly,

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:03.630
named the entertainment
industry task force,

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:06.090
and its mission, which everyone accepted,

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.190
was to help America prevent another 9/11

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:10.170
by brainstorming terrorist scenarios

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.240
so out of the box crazy,

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.250
they could only happen in the movies,

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:17.880
unless, that is, Al-Qaeda
thought of them first.

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:19.830
The first meeting began
at around seven pm,

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:21.960
didn't break up until
sometime before midnight.

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:24.420
Attendees had so many
horrifyingly destructive

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:25.980
and diabolical ideas,

00:25:25.980 --> 00:25:27.750
they decided to meet
again a few weeks later

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.310
for a second session.

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:30.960
All signed non-disclosure agreements

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:32.340
swearing them to secrecy,

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:34.320
and while there were whispers
in Hollywood newsrooms

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:37.593
about some sort of hush-hush
summit, nobody blabbed a word.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.340
I want to believe that
this wasn't serious,

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:42.870
that the high-level military officials

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.570
saw an excuse to mingle with celebrities,

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:47.520
that Hollywood's overgrown man-children

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:50.100
got their chance to playact initiation

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:52.470
into the world of covert operations,

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:54.180
that the memo that was
produced from these meetings

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:56.340
was quietly placed in
a dusty file cabinet,

00:25:56.340 --> 00:25:59.550
no doubt, with top secret
stamped on every page.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:02.790
I wanna believe that,
but I remember too well

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:05.550
the years of relentless
color-coded threat warnings

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:08.550
based on chatter about impending attacks.

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:11.490
The announcements made with great fanfare

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:13.830
that ludicrous plots had been disrupted.

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:17.400
A dirty bomber whose plan was
to place uranium in a bucket

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.000
and swing it around his
body to create a centrifuge.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:23.550
A clownish conspiracy to
topple the Sears Tower

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:26.190
into Lake Michigan to create a tsunami.

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:28.200
Vice President Dick Cheney
described that last one

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:29.523
as a very real threat.

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.960
A key ingredient in the
shrill fearmongering

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:37.140
that has characterized the
post 9/11 security regime

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:39.840
is a deep desire on the
part of its participants

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:44.160
to identify as actors in an epical event.

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.660
One could fairly describe this

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:48.300
as a form of identity politics.

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:50.520
As George Packer wrote
a decade after 9/11,

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:53.137
with perhaps some measure of mea culpa,

00:26:53.137 --> 00:26:54.240
"At the time of the attacks,

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:56.610
few educated Americans born after 1950

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.640
had any direct experience
of war or persecution

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.140
or a cataclysmic failure.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:04.410
After 9/11, this gap in the
resumes of intellectuals

00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:06.870
gave them both a sense of inadequacy,

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:10.020
an outbreak of envy for
the greatest generation,

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:12.960
and a compensatory tendency
to inflate the drama

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.537
of the war on terror and
their own role in it."

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.650
In a lecture in this room six years ago,

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:21.810
I discussed the way the identity needs

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:23.100
of these would-be warriors

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:26.730
have created an inverted
politics of national security.

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:28.500
Those who proclaim that today's terrorists

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:31.980
represent an unprecedented
threat to our way of life,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.200
that our existing laws,
courts, and institutions,

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:37.230
even our prisons, are inadequate
in the face of this threat,

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:40.590
that we have no choice but to
dispense with core principles,

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:43.290
including even the
prohibition against torture,

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:44.940
to defeat this ruthless enemy.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.640
That in short, 9/11 changed everything.

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:49.740
It is these fantasists who are extolled

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:51.480
as hard-nosed realists,

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:54.480
the warriors who are willing
to take the gloves off.

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:56.460
By contrast, those who defend

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:58.920
the vitality and viability
of our constitutional system,

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.290
who insist that our existing institutions

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:05.537
are equal to the challenges
posed by transnational terrorism

00:28:05.537 --> 00:28:07.620
who demand that we abide
by core commitments,

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:11.040
including fair trials for and
humane treatment of prisoners.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:14.460
It is these cool-headed
un-panicked persons

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:17.400
who are dismissed as weak and naive,

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:19.983
afflicted by a pre-9/11 mindset.

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:22.950
That distorted worldview

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:26.400
allows the judiciary to view
its own abdication of oversight

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.440
as an expression of solidarity.

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.930
Solidarity with the strong and the wise

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:33.480
rather than professional weakness.

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:36.210
In case after case, I and my colleagues

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:38.790
encountered federal judges
who were astonished,

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:41.280
sometimes even angered, by our suggestion

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:42.540
that they carefully scrutinize

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:45.210
the claims of national security officials.

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:46.953
One case stands out.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:52.920
My ACLU colleagues challenged
the constitutionality of a law

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:54.810
that authorized the government to monitor

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:57.930
Americans' international phone
calls and emails en masse,

00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:00.210
so long as the targets of the surveillance

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.940
were not US persons, and
were reasonably believed

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:05.250
to be outside the United States.

00:29:05.250 --> 00:29:07.680
The plaintiffs were lawyers, journalists,

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:10.200
human rights researchers, and
others, whose communications

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.810
were very likely to be
swept up under the law,

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:14.790
and who had been compelled
to take burdensome steps

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:18.630
to protect the confidentiality
of sensitive information.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:21.690
Naturally, the question before
the courts was not whether

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:24.480
the constitution permitted
this mass surveillance program,

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:27.330
but whether our plaintiffs
were permitted to challenge it.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:28.860
The government moved for summary judgment

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.140
on the ground that
plaintiffs lacked standing

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:32.310
because they couldn't show

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:34.560
that their own communications
had been surveilled.

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.420
This is a standard catch 22,

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:39.360
in challenges to National
Security surveillance laws.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:41.490
Even if the law's scope is public,

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.530
its specific targets are secret,

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.480
so the government can
prevent any plaintiff

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:47.313
from testing its constitutionality.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.270
A federal district judge
agreed with the government,

00:29:51.270 --> 00:29:52.230
but a three-judge panel

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:53.640
of the Second Circuit Court of Appeals,

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:57.090
including Yale's own
Guido Calabresi, reversed,

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:58.740
holding that the
plaintiffs had demonstrated

00:29:58.740 --> 00:29:59.940
a reasonable fear

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:01.470
that their communications
would be monitored,

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:03.903
which was sufficient
to establish standing.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.320
The full Second Circuit then split

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.510
six to six on the question.

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:12.270
With six judges agreeing that
our case could go forward,

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:15.330
and the other six siding
with the government.

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:17.070
Judge Dennis Jacobs joined his colleagues

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:19.290
who believed the lawsuit
should be dismissed,

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:20.850
but he wrote separately to convey

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:24.660
just how offended he was
at being asked to decide.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:27.750
The lawsuit's allegations were inadequate,

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:30.450
implausible, and illusory, he wrote.

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:31.890
The challenged law concerned

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.510
only the surveillance of persons abroad

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:35.820
who are not United States
citizens or residents.

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:38.227
So the claim that plaintiffs
might be implicated quote,

00:30:38.227 --> 00:30:41.190
"Bears similarity to a pro
se plaintiff's allegation

00:30:41.190 --> 00:30:42.600
that the CIA is controlling him

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.660
through a radio embedded in his molar.

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:48.030
At the risk of being
obvious," he continued,

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:51.517
The purpose of this lawsuit is
litigation for its own sake.

00:30:51.517 --> 00:30:54.090
"For these lawyers to claim
a role in policy making

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:56.370
for which they were not
appointed or elected.

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:58.590
For which they are no
fitted by experience,

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:00.960
and for which they are not accountable.

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:04.080
As best I can see, the only
purpose of this litigation

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:05.220
is for counsel and plaintiffs

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:07.860
to act out their fantasy of persecution,

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:11.130
to validate their pretensions
to policy expertise,

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:14.040
to make themselves consequential
rather than marginal,

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:16.620
and to raise funds for
self-sustaining litigation.

00:31:16.620 --> 00:31:19.860
In short, counsel's and plaintiffs
only perceptible interest

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:21.090
is to carve out for themselves

00:31:21.090 --> 00:31:23.220
an influence over government policy,

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:27.030
an interest that the law
of standing forecloses."

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:29.373
Two things are striking about this tirade.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:31.403
The first is that

00:31:31.403 --> 00:31:33.990
the lawyers were not claiming
a role for themselves.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:37.140
They were trying to claim
a role for Judge Jacobs.

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:40.730
Congress had enacted a law,
the president had signed it,

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.240
in asking a court to determine

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:43.410
whether the political branches

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.810
had overstepped constitutional boundaries,

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:48.360
the ACLU was not usurping
a policymaking role.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.493
We were invoking a judicial one.

00:31:51.420 --> 00:31:54.210
The second is that the ACLU
and our plaintiffs were right,

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.900
and Judge Jacobs quite wrong
about the law in question.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.960
We did not learn this through
the litigation, of course.

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:03.150
The Supreme Court sided with Judge Jacobs

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:06.180
ending the case before it could begin.

00:32:06.180 --> 00:32:09.330
We learned it through a
spectacular act of law breaking.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:10.440
When Edward Snowden shared

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:13.080
classified documents with journalists.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:13.950
It turned out that under

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:15.570
the putative authority
of the challenged law,

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:19.440
the NSA had engaged in what it
calls upstream surveillance,

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:21.510
which involves placing
surveillance equipment

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:22.980
on the internet's central arteries,

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.590
and systematically
intercepting and copying

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:28.950
private email messages,
phone calls, and other data

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:30.510
flowing in and out of the country,

00:32:30.510 --> 00:32:32.970
and then searching that data
with thousands of keywords

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:35.790
that are never approved by any judge.

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:37.920
Even the government's more
targeted use of the law

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:41.190
to obtain the communications
of specific foreigners

00:32:41.190 --> 00:32:43.920
swept up the communications
of many Americans.

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:47.430
In July of 2014, the "Washington Post"

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:49.800
published a deeply reported investigation

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:52.050
into surveillance under the 2008 law

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:57.000
based on a cache of 160,000
intercepted communications.

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.100
Ordinary internet users far outnumber

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:01.470
illegally targeted foreigners,
the "Post" revealed,

00:33:01.470 --> 00:33:03.300
and nearly half of the surveillance files,

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.611
a strikingly high proportion,

00:33:05.611 --> 00:33:07.620
contained names, email
addresses, or other details

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.509
that the NSA marked

00:33:09.509 --> 00:33:12.570
as belonging to US citizens or residents.

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:16.590
Our plaintiff's allegations
were very far from implausible.

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:20.490
We strongly suspected, but
could not know that at the time,

00:33:20.490 --> 00:33:21.813
but some observers knew.

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:24.750
One of them was Edward Snowden,

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.000
who saw the Supreme Court's
dismissal of our case

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.850
on justiciability grounds
as a final piece of evidence

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:33.300
that national security checks
and balances were broken.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.160
Less than three months later,

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:37.920
he walked out of an NSA facility in Hawaii

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.880
carrying the evidence that
courts had refused to look at.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:44.340
The irony is that had the federal courts

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:46.500
deigned to consider our claims

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.290
rather than dismiss them
without adjudication,

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:50.670
they might have prevented

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.130
the most consequential unauthorized leak

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:54.330
in the nation's history.

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:00.630
In June of 2013, when "The
Guardian" and "Washington Post"

00:34:00.630 --> 00:34:02.490
published their first blockbuster stories

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:05.370
based on documents provided
by Edwards Snowden,

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:07.837
President Obama responded testily,

00:34:07.837 --> 00:34:09.600
"The newly disclosed
surveillance programs,

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:10.740
including the revelation

00:34:10.740 --> 00:34:12.480
that the government
was collecting metadata

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:17.480
from virtually all US phone
calls on an ongoing daily basis

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:20.550
should be of no concern
to Americans because,"

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:22.500
the President insisted,
"they were conducted

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:24.570
under very strict supervision

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:26.940
by all three branches of government.

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:29.370
We've got congressional oversight
and judicial oversight,"

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.290
he said, "and if people can't trust

00:34:31.290 --> 00:34:34.590
not only the executive branch,
but also don't trust Congress

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:36.420
and don't trust federal judges

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:38.280
to make sure that we're
abiding by the Constitution

00:34:38.280 --> 00:34:39.960
due process and rule of law,

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.210
then we're going to have
some problems here."

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:45.660
The president's claim that all
three branches of government

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:48.180
were involved in monitoring
the surveillance activities

00:34:48.180 --> 00:34:51.840
was generally accurate, but
far from being a defense,

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:53.940
it would prove to be a deep
source of embarrassment

00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:55.560
for anyone who had argued

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:57.870
that legal oversight of
national security programs

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:00.570
was functional and effective.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:01.650
That's because the activities

00:35:01.650 --> 00:35:02.880
that had been approved in secret

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:06.000
failed to survive public scrutiny.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:08.220
Stung by sunlight, all
three branches of government

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:12.090
felt compelled to change
course, sometimes dramatically.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:13.440
The president appointed a panel

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:15.630
of senior intelligence
officials and legal experts

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:17.760
to conduct a comprehensive review

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:19.260
of intelligence surveillance practices,

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:22.500
and the panel recommended
46 sweeping reforms,

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:23.520
some of which were adopted

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:26.640
just months after the president's remarks.

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.340
For the first and only time
in my two decades at the ACLU,

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.920
the federal courts did
not dismiss our lawsuit

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:33.060
challenging the legality

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:34.650
of an intelligence surveillance program

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:36.780
on standing or secrecy grounds.

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:38.820
Instead, the Second Circuit
Court of Appeals ruled

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:42.570
that the NSA's mass collection
of Americans' call records

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:45.483
was illegal, and had always been illegal,

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:48.570
And for the first time since the 1970s,

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:51.240
Congress enacted legislation that reduced

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:53.820
rather than expanded the
surveillance authorities

00:35:53.820 --> 00:35:55.500
of the intelligence community.

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:58.110
No one has plausibly argued

00:35:58.110 --> 00:36:00.570
that any of these
reforms would've occurred

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:01.770
if Snowden had not shared

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:04.200
classified documents with the press.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:07.350
I think some of the
condescension and disdain

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:10.530
with which Snowden is viewed
in national security circles

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:12.240
is attributable to the awkward fact

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:13.950
that the only significant reforms

00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:17.040
to intelligence surveillance
laws in modern American history

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:19.830
have been initiated by
leaks and law breaking,

00:36:19.830 --> 00:36:23.940
not by the system's supposedly
rigorous checks and balances.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.090
That's why I have little
patience for those who scold

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:29.190
that Snowden should
have raised his concerns

00:36:29.190 --> 00:36:30.810
through the system.

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:33.510
What system do people think
they're talking about?

00:36:33.510 --> 00:36:34.500
What's the proper channel

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:36.240
for reporting that the
intelligence community

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:39.090
developed and deployed a
global mass surveillance system

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.490
with the blessing of all
three branches of government,

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:43.113
but without public consultation?

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:46.620
The truth is that Snowden did vastly more

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:48.540
to reform intelligence
surveillance practices

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:49.713
by breaking the law,

00:36:49.713 --> 00:36:51.720
than I and my colleagues did

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:54.453
by requesting politely
that courts enforce it.

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:58.140
I regard this as a very grave indictment

00:36:58.140 --> 00:37:01.380
of national security law and practice.

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:04.320
Is it also an indictment of
public interest litigation

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:06.390
aimed at constraining
national security abuses,

00:37:06.390 --> 00:37:09.870
albeit quite a different one
than Judge Jacobs offered?

00:37:09.870 --> 00:37:11.910
Did all of this failed litigation,

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:14.220
all of these dismissals
on justiciability grounds

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:17.130
unwittingly enhance the
prestige of the system,

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:21.000
and help reify the status
of national security law?

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:23.130
There's no question
that I and my colleagues

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:25.710
were actors in this theater of law.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:29.370
And one could reasonably
argue that our participation

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:34.260
conferred undeserved legitimacy
on the whole sordid charade.

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:37.230
Yale's estimable Sam Moyn,
who is with us tonight,

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:39.480
has offered a different
version of that argument,

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:41.520
suggesting that the
litigation I've described

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:45.240
had the effect of making the
US's global war more sanitized

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:47.400
and thereby more sustainable.

00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:49.710
Instead of litigating to
challenge the legality of war,

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:51.840
we chose to, in Moyn's words,

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:53.880
defend the rights of those swept up,

00:37:53.880 --> 00:37:56.520
and in so doing may have
laundered the inhumanity

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.290
from what began as a brutal enterprise

00:37:58.290 --> 00:37:59.850
by helping to recodify a war

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:02.883
that thus became endless,
legal, and humane.

00:38:04.230 --> 00:38:07.110
In general, I find any suggestion

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:08.820
that my time could have been better spent

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:11.253
almost irresistibly compelling.

00:38:11.253 --> 00:38:14.490
(audience laughing)

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:16.350
While much of our litigation did in fact

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:17.550
challenge the war paradigm,

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:19.740
I am not here to argue
that the cases I brought,

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:20.970
and the causes I championed

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:23.430
materially changed the
world for the better.

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:25.650
Still, what I find confounding
about Moyn's argument

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:27.780
is the suggestion that the decisions

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:29.190
of a handful of human rights lawyers

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:31.620
had any geopolitical significance,

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:32.460
and the implication

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:36.120
that had we devoted our
energies elsewhere or nowhere,

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:38.820
the course of the forever
war might have been altered,

00:38:39.885 --> 00:38:41.535
or its foreverness foreshortened.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:44.940
Whether public interest
litigation after 9/11

00:38:44.940 --> 00:38:47.160
did more to legitimize or de-legitimize

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:48.990
national security law
and the war on terror,

00:38:48.990 --> 00:38:51.180
will depend in the final analysis

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:52.860
on whether and how the legal community,

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:54.690
including the legal academy,

00:38:54.690 --> 00:38:58.710
responds to the record of
impunity I've laid out here.

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:02.100
Will it continue to confer
prestige and dignity

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:04.200
on a regime that deems non-justiciable

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:06.780
some of the most vital questions
concerning the relationship

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:10.410
between state power and individual rights?

00:39:10.410 --> 00:39:12.780
Or might the embarrassments
of these last two decades

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:15.090
compel the emergence of competing norms

00:39:15.090 --> 00:39:18.030
that bring some measure of
democratic accountability

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:19.920
into this place of exception?

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:22.113
What would those norms look like?

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:26.910
First and foremost, the
doctrine of judicial deference

00:39:26.910 --> 00:39:30.510
to executive branch expertise
has slipped its moorings,

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:34.290
and is now deployed, not to
respect separation of powers,

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:35.253
but to erode them.

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:38.040
The notion that courts are incompetent,

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:40.923
but the CIA is competent,
has always struck me as odd.

00:39:41.970 --> 00:39:44.310
But even if there are
appropriately cabin instances

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:45.510
where deference is warranted,

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:48.270
it's judges, not leaders of spy agencies,

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:50.130
who possess the institutional competence

00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:52.410
to weigh competing interests.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:54.060
Courts should be especially on guard

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:56.310
for the structural conflicts
of interest that arise

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:57.930
when the putative expert

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:01.230
also represents the entity
accused of wrongdoing.

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:02.130
In those instances,

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:04.050
courts would do better to
begin with the assumption

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:06.303
that the executive is not credible.

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:10.380
Some years ago I was
invited to share a stage

00:40:10.380 --> 00:40:13.200
at a judicial conference
with the late John Rizzo,

00:40:13.200 --> 00:40:15.870
who had been the CIA's
acting general counsel

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:18.870
during the post-9/11 torture conspiracy.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:20.340
The moderator of our polite debate

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:22.290
was none other than Judge Dennis Jacobs,

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:24.540
who is more gracious in
person than in print.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:28.170
I don't remember much
about the event itself,

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:30.060
but I spent a fair amount of time

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:32.160
with John Rizzo over that weekend

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:34.860
as he smoked cigars at
an outdoor picnic table,

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:37.710
and held forth on the political
and intelligence leaders

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.580
he'd crossed paths with
during 34 years at the CIA.

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:43.860
This one was good for the agency.

00:40:43.860 --> 00:40:46.320
That one hung us out to dry.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:49.350
This other one was very
good for the agency.

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:53.340
The man unapologetically divided
the world into two camps.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:54.870
That's understandable.

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:56.610
He didn't want his
friends to go to prison,

00:40:56.610 --> 00:40:58.260
or have their careers derailed.

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:00.990
We'd be naive to expect otherwise,

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:02.880
but why in the world would we want judges

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:04.950
to defer to someone like this?

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:06.900
If John Rizzo was an expert in anything,

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:08.490
it was in keeping himself and his people

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:10.950
out of legal and political trouble.

00:41:10.950 --> 00:41:13.050
All too often, demands for deference

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:15.270
have nothing to do with expertise.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:16.110
Rather, they're demands

00:41:16.110 --> 00:41:19.473
that courts treat national
security claims as absolute.

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:21.840
If the CIA director insists

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:24.330
that allowing a torture
victim his day in court

00:41:24.330 --> 00:41:26.220
will complicate the agency's relationship

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:28.290
with the foreign intelligence service,

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:30.000
how that interest should be balanced

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:31.860
against other important interests,

00:41:31.860 --> 00:41:34.530
such as providing a remedy
for an abuse victim,

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:37.170
or ensuring accountability for misconduct,

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.200
or resolving a
consequential legal question

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:42.330
is quintessentially a judicial function,

00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:44.070
not a security function.

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:46.530
If courts refuse under
the banner of deference

00:41:46.530 --> 00:41:48.060
to claim that role,

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:50.430
national security officials
will continue to be

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.463
the expert architects
of their own impunity.

00:41:56.100 --> 00:41:58.320
The uses and misuses of secrecy,

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:01.380
which I've spoken about at
length these last two days,

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:04.620
present a distinct, but related challenge.

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.453
As we've discussed,

00:42:05.453 --> 00:42:08.010
the government routinely
invokes secrecy doctrines

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:09.450
to shield from legal scrutiny

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:11.820
activities that are not secret at all.

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:14.580
The so-called official
acknowledgement doctrine, which holds

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:17.160
that even public information
is treated as secret

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:19.980
so long as the government
chooses not to confirm it,

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:22.623
has become an insurmountable
liability shield.

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:27.690
This is an accountability crisis
of the court's own making,

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:29.760
and the solution is straightforward.

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.754
Courts should stop treating

00:42:31.754 --> 00:42:32.670
the government's asserted interests

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:35.190
as an all or nothing proposition.

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:37.530
They can accept that
there may be situations

00:42:37.530 --> 00:42:40.500
in which official
confirmation of known facts

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:42.060
creates a distinct harm,

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:45.210
but not accept that the
judicial inquiry ends there,

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:47.280
if core values are at stake.

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:50.070
In other words, they can introduce
proportionality analysis,

00:42:50.070 --> 00:42:52.260
which is very common
in other jurisdictions,

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:54.150
but has been completely alien

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:57.630
to US national security law in practice.

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:00.330
Even when secrets are
still actually secret,

00:43:00.330 --> 00:43:04.650
courts have a vital role for
reasons that should be obvious.

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:06.568
Excessive deference

00:43:06.568 --> 00:43:08.100
to executive branch
classification decisions

00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:11.190
threatens any theory of
democratic governance.

00:43:11.190 --> 00:43:13.050
If the president and his designees

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:15.150
have complete control over the information

00:43:15.150 --> 00:43:17.250
by which we can judge their actions,

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.020
then the public can neither consent to

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:20.680
nor hold leaders accountable

00:43:21.535 --> 00:43:24.003
for actions undertaken in our name.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:27.750
At oral argument in the FOIA
case I discussed yesterday,

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:30.810
one judge challenged
me to cite prior cases

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:32.340
in which courts had overridden

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.470
executive branch classification decisions

00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:36.840
in national security litigation.

00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:40.050
I could not get past the
fingers on my first hand.

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:43.470
It's not hard to understand
judges' reluctance

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:46.200
when an executive branch security officer

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:47.760
shows up in their chambers,

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:51.180
with a briefcase handcuffed to his wrist,

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:52.980
and delivers a classified affidavit

00:43:52.980 --> 00:43:55.320
from the director of central intelligence

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:57.480
attesting to the glaciers that will melt

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:59.550
and the oceans that will boil off

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:01.440
if a case is allowed to go forward.

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:05.910
Very rarely will a judge be
inclined to dig any deeper,

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:08.040
but they should dig deeper,

00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:09.600
and they should consider these claims

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:11.850
against the backdrop of a long history

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.960
of gross exaggerations
and outright deceptions,

00:44:15.960 --> 00:44:17.610
and they should do so with confidence.

00:44:17.610 --> 00:44:21.030
The only people whose security
is likely to be jeopardized

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:24.633
are those who misuse secrecy
to conceal misconduct.

00:44:26.460 --> 00:44:28.320
None of these changes in judicial behavior

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.580
are likely to occur,

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:33.690
absent a broader societal
shift towards resilience.

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:36.420
We've allowed rhetorical
excesses about a war on terrorism

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:37.710
to obscure the distinction

00:44:37.710 --> 00:44:41.220
between acceptable and unacceptable risks.

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:44.580
Justice Brennan hoped that
judges would develop the capacity

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:46.140
to make those distinctions,

00:44:46.140 --> 00:44:48.120
but judges are far more likely to reflect

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:51.270
society's relationship
to risk than to guide it.

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:53.820
The amorphous term national security,

00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:56.010
which we apply indiscriminately

00:44:56.010 --> 00:44:59.340
to efforts to prevent nuclear
proliferation on the one hand

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:01.800
and the arrest of inept knuckleheads

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:03.360
entrapped by FBI informants

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:05.880
into implausible terrorist
plots on the other hand,

00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:07.260
isn't helpful.

00:45:07.260 --> 00:45:10.710
The former is a legitimate
matter of national defense.

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:12.030
The latter should not be regarded

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:13.710
as a matter of any national importance,

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:15.540
much less national security.

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:18.360
We should scorn, rather than
aggrandize, political leaders

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:20.700
who refuse to acknowledge
that distinction,

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.280
and who equate the danger
of terrorism with world wars

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:25.800
in which millions of
lives were extinguished,

00:45:25.800 --> 00:45:27.450
or a Cold war in which billions lived

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:28.900
under threat of annihilation.

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:33.180
My sense is that most Americans

00:45:33.180 --> 00:45:35.673
spend very little time
thinking about terrorism.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:40.710
And that institutional
incentives, not public fears,

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:41.580
best account

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:45.030
for how the most secure
society in human history

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:48.000
is kept in a state of legal emergency.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:50.460
Those incentives will
be difficult to disrupt,

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.410
but we have to start somewhere.

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:54.630
And if any institution
should be at the forefront

00:45:54.630 --> 00:45:58.500
of reasserting some sense of
moral poise rather than panic

00:45:58.500 --> 00:46:01.980
in the face of ineradicable,
but non-existential threats,

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:03.153
it's the legal academy.

00:46:06.750 --> 00:46:09.270
For all the ways in which the 9/11 attacks

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.690
reshaped our law and institutions,

00:46:12.690 --> 00:46:16.590
the silence and shade that
still surround our efforts

00:46:16.590 --> 00:46:19.140
to bring the alleged
perpetrators to justice

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:21.420
are truly remarkable.

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:25.350
This is the signal embarrassment
of national security law,

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:28.500
that in the 20-odd
years since the attacks,

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.990
the US has managed to
invade two countries,

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:33.270
and send our military into many others

00:46:33.270 --> 00:46:34.890
to construct and then dismantle

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:36.690
a secret global detention apparatus,

00:46:36.690 --> 00:46:39.300
to develop and deploy
systems of mass surveillance,

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.790
to create new domestic security agencies,

00:46:41.790 --> 00:46:44.910
but has failed even to begin the trial

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:46.290
of the five alleged perpetrators

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:49.260
who have been in our
custody for two decades.

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:50.970
This might make an ideal starting point

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:53.220
for a course on national security law.

00:46:53.220 --> 00:46:55.720
One that could not be
taught in a single sentence.

00:46:57.180 --> 00:46:58.170
The course could explore

00:46:58.170 --> 00:47:02.160
how grave misjudgments become
self-fulfilling prophecies

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:04.980
through a kind of bad faith syllogism.

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.680
First we decide that our
existing laws and institutions

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:08.862
cannot.

00:47:08.862 --> 00:47:10.545
- [Cell Phone] Those ads
really drive you mad, the game.

00:47:10.545 --> 00:47:11.813
(audience laughing)

00:47:11.813 --> 00:47:12.755
- [Participant] Sorry.

00:47:12.755 --> 00:47:14.220
- That's okay.

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:16.710
First, we decide that our
existing laws and institutions

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:18.870
can't accommodate the
detention, interrogation,

00:47:18.870 --> 00:47:22.413
and trial of these prisoners,
so new rules are needed.

00:47:23.250 --> 00:47:24.570
Next, we subject the prisoners

00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:26.250
to such appalling mistreatment

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:27.960
that convictions under our existing laws

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:31.110
may in fact be impracticable,

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:34.530
but because we won't release
them under any circumstances,

00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:36.210
we now need new rules

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:39.240
that will allow us to adjudicate
their crimes against us

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:42.273
while suppressing our crimes against them.

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:45.270
That these new rules constitute

00:47:45.270 --> 00:47:47.490
a moral, practical, and legal disaster

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:49.530
that have caused decades of delays,

00:47:49.530 --> 00:47:52.710
is almost beside the
point to their proponents.

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:56.490
The point of the rules was
impunity for the rule makers,

00:47:56.490 --> 00:47:58.410
and it's precisely that impunity

00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:01.833
that will make this paradigm
so difficult to dislodge.

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:05.400
If we're going to be
stuck in this paradigm,

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:07.473
as it appears we will be for some time,

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:11.460
let's at least insist on a
definition of national security

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:12.750
that encompasses more

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:16.380
than the never-ending menu
of threats against us.

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:17.460
Let's agree that a system

00:48:17.460 --> 00:48:21.480
that deems the perpetrators
of grave abuses above the law,

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:24.870
and the victims of
those abuses beneath it,

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:26.673
should not be called secure.

00:48:27.570 --> 00:48:31.020
And let's resolve that the
security of senior officials,

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:32.970
the architects and beneficiaries

00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:36.510
of these states of exception,
will no longer be attained

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:40.023
at the expense of the security
of our laws and values.

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:42.873
Thank you very much for listening.

00:48:42.873 --> 00:48:46.040
(audience applauding)

00:48:53.977 --> 00:48:55.170
- [Participant] Thank you so much.

00:48:55.170 --> 00:48:57.480
I'm wondering.
- Nice to see you again.

00:48:57.480 --> 00:48:58.313
- [Participant] Nice to be back.

00:48:58.313 --> 00:49:02.370
I'm wondering how you see
the national security regime,

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:04.680
as you call it, as having changed at all.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:07.770
It seems our political
leaders have sort of moved on,

00:49:07.770 --> 00:49:11.280
at least rhetorically,
from the war on terror.

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:13.800
So have you seen any sort of developments

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:15.510
in this national insecurity regime?

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:16.920
- Yeah, I mean one of the cases that,

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:17.850
thank you for that question.

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:20.100
One of the cases that
I discussed yesterday,

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:23.697
was so strange because
it was 2021 or 2022,

00:49:23.697 --> 00:49:26.670
and the Supreme Court was
talking about Abu Zubayda,

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:28.410
and it was like, wow, we're
still talking about this,

00:49:28.410 --> 00:49:30.120
and we're still saying

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:33.360
that what happened to Abu
Zubayda in 2002 or 2003

00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:34.890
that everyone knows is a state secret.

00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:37.890
So in one sense the
system has not moved on.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:41.880
In another sense, you
know, I said yesterday

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:46.650
that this system is adept
at identifying new hosts.

00:49:46.650 --> 00:49:49.200
I think sort of seeing the pivot

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:54.030
that the national security
set has made, you know,

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:56.770
to being self-styled
defenders of democracy

00:49:58.350 --> 00:50:02.730
in response to Russian
propaganda, for example.

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:05.268
You know, now you have the war in Ukraine.

00:50:05.268 --> 00:50:08.130
The whole cybersecurity issue writ large.

00:50:08.130 --> 00:50:12.720
There's always an effort to,
you know, identify something

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:14.880
that will continue to justify

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:16.980
the massive budgets and authorities,

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:18.990
and that will require the secrecy

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:20.790
that is the keystone for that impunity.

00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:22.080
So I agree with you

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:25.320
that there's not as much
conversation about terrorism

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:28.470
in part because we haven't
had much terrorism here,

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:31.532
and most Americans are
not talking about that.

00:50:31.532 --> 00:50:34.230
But you certainly have
not seen any reduction

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:36.630
in the budgets of the
agencies that were created

00:50:37.950 --> 00:50:42.130
to take this on, or really
any crack in the wall

00:50:43.405 --> 00:50:46.503
of the kind of legal black hole
that the system represents.

00:50:50.611 --> 00:50:53.103
- [Anna] Do you mind?

00:50:56.370 --> 00:50:59.103
- [Hope] So thank you. That was great, hi.

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:02.490
I guess I had a similar question,

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:04.165
which is to ask you to

00:51:04.165 --> 00:51:06.930
do a little bit of forecasting for us.

00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:10.440
So since the years that you
and I litigated together,

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:15.440
I've done more work at the
international sort of level,

00:51:16.680 --> 00:51:19.350
and something that's really striking

00:51:19.350 --> 00:51:23.790
is that the US creates frameworks,

00:51:23.790 --> 00:51:26.580
and in this case, plausible deniability

00:51:26.580 --> 00:51:28.290
that of course then becomes a model

00:51:28.290 --> 00:51:30.330
for many, many other governments,

00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:33.363
and you know, in other places,

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:37.170
El Salvador being a notable example,

00:51:37.170 --> 00:51:39.780
the National Security
framework now is being used

00:51:39.780 --> 00:51:42.540
to go after street level gang leaders.

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:45.150
So I'm just curious if from
your view that, you know,

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:50.150
you talked about it being
a sort of a paradigm

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:53.340
that is able to find
many ghosts but hosts,

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:54.900
but I hope that you could just say

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:56.400
a little bit more about that.

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:59.130
And whether, and sorry,
and I guess just relatedly,

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:01.470
whether you think that that potentially

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:06.470
is a way in which the
politics around this change,

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:11.990
because one of the problems
in terms of mobilizing

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:16.260
support for doing the kind of challenges

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:17.880
that we've tried to do

00:52:17.880 --> 00:52:21.420
is the fact that the people
were not only despised,

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:24.600
but they had no political
constituency within this country.

00:52:24.600 --> 00:52:26.070
- Yeah, and so, you know, as you know,

00:52:26.070 --> 00:52:30.030
I went from representing
torture victims in torture cases

00:52:30.030 --> 00:52:31.530
where basically everybody agreed,

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:33.120
every reasonable person
agreed it was a problem,

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:35.490
but didn't think it had
any connection to them.

00:52:35.490 --> 00:52:37.620
To working on issues of mass surveillance,

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:39.180
where most people didn't
think it was a problem,

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:41.490
but they all realized it was
connected to them in some way.

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:45.440
Right, but there, there
was an opportunity, I mean,

00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:48.750
you know, you did have
significant political majorities

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:50.760
that were not partisan majorities,

00:52:50.760 --> 00:52:52.590
that were pretty uncomfortable

00:52:52.590 --> 00:52:54.870
with the revelations that came
out of the Snowden regime.

00:52:54.870 --> 00:52:55.740
I mean, the reason why

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:57.450
the branches of government changed course

00:52:57.450 --> 00:53:01.290
was because the public wanted that change.

00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:02.910
So I do think that, you know,

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:07.910
to the extent that the government
abuses touch more people,

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:09.900
they create more opportunities

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:11.610
to change the political discourse.

00:53:11.610 --> 00:53:12.900
You know, sort of looking overseas,

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:14.670
I would just say, I mean, as you remember,

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:17.520
there were clients who
we represented together

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:20.190
who were citizens of other countries.

00:53:20.190 --> 00:53:22.290
The US got our cases dismissed

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:23.580
on the grounds that it would complicate

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:25.020
their relationship with those countries.

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:26.280
Meanwhile, the courts in those countries

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:28.080
gave remedies to our clients.

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:31.253
Right, and used other kinds
of analysis, and you know,

00:53:31.253 --> 00:53:32.220
even even if they didn't
wanna litigate the cases

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:34.410
and expose their official secrets,

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:37.530
they realized that that meant
they should pay the victims

00:53:37.530 --> 00:53:40.080
and not, you know, have this incentive

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:42.690
where they simultaneously
get to claim secrecy

00:53:42.690 --> 00:53:44.010
and toss the victim out of court.

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:49.010
So one of the very frustrating
things about, you know,

00:53:49.380 --> 00:53:51.810
doing human rights work in a US framework

00:53:51.810 --> 00:53:56.520
is how little interest,
you know, US courts have

00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:00.390
in being in conversation with
courts in other democracies

00:54:00.390 --> 00:54:02.130
that face greater terrorism
threats than we do.

00:54:02.130 --> 00:54:04.830
And yet find a way to adjudicate

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:06.680
some of these same kinds of disputes.

00:54:20.370 --> 00:54:24.090
- [Participant] A totally
brilliant talk, so well delivered.

00:54:24.090 --> 00:54:29.090
I just wanna, you know, hear
more about Edward Snowden,

00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:32.220
'cause I totally accept
your critique of me,

00:54:32.220 --> 00:54:36.840
but it seemed like you accepted
like a deep self-critique

00:54:36.840 --> 00:54:39.450
in addressing what
Edward Snowden got wrong.

00:54:39.450 --> 00:54:42.061
So I guess I'm a little perplexed.

00:54:42.061 --> 00:54:43.770
- Wait, what he got
wrong, what he got right.

00:54:43.770 --> 00:54:44.850
- [Participant] What he got right, sorry.

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:46.400
- Yeah, yeah.

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:48.374
So I accept, the self-critique
I'm really good at.

00:54:48.374 --> 00:54:50.021
- [Participant] Yeah, so I just want,

00:54:50.021 --> 00:54:52.410
I'm just trying to understand
the kind of credible pathway

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:54.480
to political change in the situation,

00:54:54.480 --> 00:54:59.480
because you've said the judges,
we spent, you know, decades

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:03.360
we're still doing it,
asking judges for change,

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:05.520
which they will never deliver.

00:55:05.520 --> 00:55:07.260
At the same time, you engaged

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:10.560
in a lot of idealization
of the constitution itself,

00:55:10.560 --> 00:55:15.560
of the legal system, and
that's part of your shtick,

00:55:16.950 --> 00:55:21.540
even as you acknowledge
that they're the problem.

00:55:21.540 --> 00:55:24.150
And then Snowden makes more change

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:27.900
than legal activism ever
does by breaking the law.

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:32.460
So I guess I don't understand
what the political move is.

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:36.480
- It's a great question,
and it is a paradox.

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:40.050
Because the way that
Snowden creates change

00:55:40.050 --> 00:55:42.030
is by breaking the law,

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:46.230
and thereby reinvigorating
legal and democratic oversight.

00:55:46.230 --> 00:55:50.130
We basically only get energetic oversight,

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:51.600
judicial or legislative,

00:55:51.600 --> 00:55:54.930
in response to leaks and law breaking.

00:55:54.930 --> 00:55:56.580
The whole modern apparatus

00:55:56.580 --> 00:56:00.510
of intelligence surveillance oversight

00:56:00.510 --> 00:56:02.610
was a result of the Church Committee,

00:56:02.610 --> 00:56:05.520
which was a result of a
bunch of anti-war activists

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:08.310
breaking into an FBI field
office outside of Philadelphia,

00:56:08.310 --> 00:56:11.220
stealing all the files, and
mailing them to Betty Metzger

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:12.750
at "The Washington Post," right.

00:56:12.750 --> 00:56:16.860
So like, basically, and
all of the, you know,

00:56:16.860 --> 00:56:18.930
sort of limited successes that we've had

00:56:18.930 --> 00:56:19.830
over the last decade,

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:22.900
you can trace to someone inside the system

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:26.550
deciding that the public
had a need to see something.

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:28.980
And then once the public
has that information,

00:56:28.980 --> 00:56:31.470
I think lawyers and even litigators

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:33.300
are actors in that, right.

00:56:33.300 --> 00:56:35.700
So we did file a successful lawsuit

00:56:35.700 --> 00:56:37.980
based on documents that Snowden gave us.

00:56:37.980 --> 00:56:40.500
It turned out that even
our unsuccessful lawsuit

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:43.350
was a critical piece of
bringing him forward.

00:56:43.350 --> 00:56:44.730
And I know not from Snowden,

00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:47.040
but from journalists
who got his documents,

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:49.290
that there were subfolders
in what he gave them

00:56:49.290 --> 00:56:51.033
that were marked for the ACLU.

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:56.520
So that kind of conversation
goes on as well.

00:56:56.520 --> 00:57:01.520
But, yeah, I take your point
that there is a, you know,

00:57:03.180 --> 00:57:08.180
tension between my
disparagement of the system,

00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:11.820
and my aggrandizement of how it should be,

00:57:11.820 --> 00:57:16.043
and I can see that it's undertheorized.

00:57:22.707 --> 00:57:24.338
- [Participant] I might just mention

00:57:24.338 --> 00:57:28.230
that the Pentagon Papers
were another example.

00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:30.630
The Pentagon Papers are another example

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:33.210
where there was an illegal release

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:35.550
then printed in "The New York Times"

00:57:35.550 --> 00:57:38.580
and which had a profound impact

00:57:38.580 --> 00:57:43.580
on the resolution of the
Vietnamese war and so forth, so.

00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:45.677
- Yeah, I mean, interestingly,

00:57:45.677 --> 00:57:47.343
Dan Ellsberg thinks it didn't,

00:57:47.343 --> 00:57:49.020
he thinks he was years too late.

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:50.730
- [Participant] Well, he may
have been years too late,

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:53.130
but there's no question that.

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:55.740
- Yeah, well, there's other
parallels, which is that the,

00:57:55.740 --> 00:57:58.860
you know, Nixon White House claimed that

00:57:58.860 --> 00:58:01.590
Ellsberg had delivered a
set of the Pentagon papers

00:58:01.590 --> 00:58:06.590
to the Soviet consulate or embassy in DC.

00:58:06.917 --> 00:58:08.767
He was viewed as a spy and a traitor.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:13.260
That was one of the cases that
I could put on my one hand

00:58:13.260 --> 00:58:15.030
where a court actually said,

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:17.130
in the face of a government secrecy claim,

00:58:17.130 --> 00:58:19.530
no, you haven't showed us enough

00:58:19.530 --> 00:58:21.990
for us to give you this injunction.

00:58:21.990 --> 00:58:24.408
And it's a good thing they did.

00:58:24.408 --> 00:58:27.150
I mentioned this last
night, but, you know,

00:58:27.150 --> 00:58:29.640
10 years after the
government lost that case

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:30.840
in the Supreme Court,

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:32.910
the Solicitor General at
the time, Erwin Griswold,

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:35.490
wrote an op-ed saying,
secret's not worth keeping.

00:58:35.490 --> 00:58:36.840
He said in the decade that had passed,

00:58:36.840 --> 00:58:39.180
he had never seen a shred of evidence

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:40.560
that anything in the Pentagon papers

00:58:40.560 --> 00:58:42.390
caused any harm to national security.

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:44.640
I had never seen anybody
suggest that it had,

00:58:45.608 --> 00:58:47.610
and that's why I say that kind of context

00:58:47.610 --> 00:58:51.450
has to be part of court's consideration

00:58:51.450 --> 00:58:52.890
of government secrecy claims,

00:58:52.890 --> 00:58:54.840
because it costs the government nothing

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:57.543
to invoke secrecy doctrines
to protect embarrassment.

00:59:04.560 --> 00:59:06.563
- [Participant] Oh,
thank you for your talk.

00:59:07.860 --> 00:59:12.860
I wanted to ask if the ACLU
has heard about, and if so,

00:59:12.930 --> 00:59:17.520
are they looking into the
following two allegations?

00:59:17.520 --> 00:59:22.520
One is that the Iranian
general who was assassinated

00:59:23.100 --> 00:59:26.610
during the Trump administration's tenure

00:59:26.610 --> 00:59:31.610
had been attending a meeting to discuss

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:36.780
a peaceful negotiation of
settlement with Saudi Arabia

00:59:36.780 --> 00:59:38.853
about the war in Yemen.

00:59:40.248 --> 00:59:43.620
Like, I mean, that's one, yeah, I mean,

00:59:43.620 --> 00:59:45.480
I could send you the source.

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:50.480
And then the second allegation
is Seymour Hirsch allegation

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:56.120
that US operatives working
in concert with Norway,

00:59:58.500 --> 01:00:03.500
I believe, blew up the
North Stream 2 pipeline

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:07.890
that would have, that was
supposedly done last year.

01:00:07.890 --> 01:00:11.073
So I just wanted to know if
you were aware of these things.

01:00:11.073 --> 01:00:11.906
And looking into them.

01:00:11.906 --> 01:00:14.656
- I'm aware of those things as a citizen.

01:00:14.656 --> 01:00:16.260
They don't fall within
my professional remit.

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:19.440
I work for a domestic civil
liberties organization

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:21.060
that does not concern itself

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:25.740
with explosions in northern waters

01:00:25.740 --> 01:00:28.830
or that kind of military operation.

01:00:28.830 --> 01:00:30.900
So I don't have anything
particular to share with you there,

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.900
and it's not, I think,
the role of journalists,

01:00:33.900 --> 01:00:35.100
not the role of the ACLU

01:00:35.100 --> 01:00:36.950
to explore those allegations further.

01:00:41.456 --> 01:00:42.520
- [Anna] Can you take the
microphone, take this real quick.

01:00:47.820 --> 01:00:49.800
- [Participant] Can I
ask, in your ideal world,

01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:53.280
what do you imagine a sustainable,

01:00:53.280 --> 01:00:55.590
like, long-term mechanism
of accountability

01:00:55.590 --> 01:00:57.420
to look like in the
national security space?

01:00:57.420 --> 01:00:59.790
Because from your talk,
it sounds like currently

01:00:59.790 --> 01:01:01.680
the only effective
mechanism of accountability

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:05.111
is relying on people to
break the law like Snowden.

01:01:05.111 --> 01:01:06.780
- Well, I mean, it's a
really hard question,

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:08.910
and I always ask when
people ask me this question,

01:01:08.910 --> 01:01:10.473
are you a computer scientist?

01:01:11.520 --> 01:01:12.353
Are you?

01:01:12.353 --> 01:01:14.657
- [Participant] Yeah, I'm a CIS agent.

01:01:14.657 --> 01:01:16.170
- Yeah, because this is the conversation

01:01:16.170 --> 01:01:18.122
I have with Snowden all the time.

01:01:18.122 --> 01:01:20.640
He thinks that democracy is
a problem that can be solved

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:22.230
because he comes from
a technical background,

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:24.360
and I tell him that it's
actually not a solvable problem,

01:01:24.360 --> 01:01:26.940
but just sort of, let
me give you an example.

01:01:26.940 --> 01:01:30.510
So the last great intelligence scandal,

01:01:30.510 --> 01:01:32.640
much bigger than the Snowden revelations,

01:01:32.640 --> 01:01:37.096
in the 1970s when we learned about

01:01:37.096 --> 01:01:38.670
the FBI's COINTELPRO program

01:01:38.670 --> 01:01:41.220
where they were sending operatives

01:01:41.220 --> 01:01:43.620
into Black student organizations
and the anti-war movement,

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:46.560
breaking up marriages, poison pen letters,

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:48.570
they tried to get Martin
Luther King to commit suicide.

01:01:48.570 --> 01:01:50.073
All of this stuff comes out,

01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:54.540
and the Congress's response
is to do three things,

01:01:54.540 --> 01:01:55.530
two things, really.

01:01:55.530 --> 01:01:56.850
So first they create

01:01:56.850 --> 01:02:00.330
the House and Senate
Intelligence Committees,

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:03.145
they decide that congressional
oversight isn't working.

01:02:03.145 --> 01:02:03.978
We need specialized committees

01:02:03.978 --> 01:02:06.180
that have specialized access
to classified information.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:09.870
We understand that the FBI
and the NSA and the CIA

01:02:09.870 --> 01:02:11.940
don't wanna share information
with everyone in Congress,

01:02:11.940 --> 01:02:15.227
'cause it will leak out, but
they can at least trust the,

01:02:15.227 --> 01:02:16.230
you know, the 10 people on
each of these committees.

01:02:16.230 --> 01:02:17.670
So we're gonna create these committees.

01:02:17.670 --> 01:02:18.960
The second thing they do is they create

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:21.570
the Foreign Intelligence
Surveillance court,

01:02:21.570 --> 01:02:26.570
which can provide some judicial
oversight of, you know,

01:02:26.880 --> 01:02:29.400
domestic intelligence surveillance,

01:02:29.400 --> 01:02:31.680
not law enforcement surveillance,
intelligence surveillance.

01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:33.330
And the interesting thing is that

01:02:34.267 --> 01:02:37.020
the key insight of the Snowden revelations

01:02:37.020 --> 01:02:39.330
was that 50 years later,

01:02:39.330 --> 01:02:44.160
those solutions to the 1970s
problem were no longer working.

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:46.050
Right, the intelligence
committees in Congress

01:02:46.050 --> 01:02:47.790
had been set up to do extra oversight.

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:49.860
It turns out they were extra captured.

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:50.790
The people on those committees

01:02:50.790 --> 01:02:52.950
were the closest ones to the
intelligence communities,

01:02:52.950 --> 01:02:55.473
not the ones who were
scrutinizing it the most.

01:02:56.373 --> 01:02:57.630
And the court, which had been created

01:02:57.630 --> 01:02:59.700
to authorize individual
intelligence warrants,

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:01.920
which is an appropriate
thing to do in secret,

01:03:01.920 --> 01:03:04.110
was instead writing 90-page opinions

01:03:04.110 --> 01:03:06.750
that were purporting to authorize
programmatic surveillance,

01:03:06.750 --> 01:03:08.130
including collecting metadata

01:03:08.130 --> 01:03:10.980
from every American's phone call every day

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:14.280
without hearing any adversarial arguments

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:16.290
from people who might say
that's not constitutional.

01:03:16.290 --> 01:03:19.380
And so what was the
solution to the Snowden?

01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:21.390
Well, now the Foreign
Intelligence Surveillance Court

01:03:21.390 --> 01:03:25.680
can invite in expert adversaries
who will come in, you know,

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:28.230
it's a tweak that was made to that system.

01:03:28.230 --> 01:03:30.210
I don't have a structural
solution here, right.

01:03:30.210 --> 01:03:34.770
I don't think that the structure is wrong.

01:03:34.770 --> 01:03:38.220
You want an architecture
that divides power

01:03:38.220 --> 01:03:40.020
between different branches of government,

01:03:40.020 --> 01:03:42.240
but you need the representatives

01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:43.470
of those branches of government

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:47.332
to take pride in that
independence and not consider it.

01:03:47.332 --> 01:03:49.290
I mean, the dominant judicial doctrine

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:54.290
in national security law is
deference to the expertise of,

01:03:54.810 --> 01:03:57.000
as I put it, the very perpetrators

01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:58.800
of these abuses themselves.

01:03:58.800 --> 01:04:03.240
And that's not an acceptable
mindset for judges to bring,

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:06.600
I didn't come here to talk to judges.

01:04:06.600 --> 01:04:10.200
You know, the much more
modest goal here is, you know,

01:04:10.200 --> 01:04:13.020
at least law schools should think about

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:16.773
what this body of law is before
they put it on a pedestal.

01:04:18.660 --> 01:04:19.583
- [Participant] Thank you.

01:04:35.400 --> 01:04:37.077
- [Participant] Hi. Thanks.

01:04:37.077 --> 01:04:38.310
I wonder if you might
share any war stories

01:04:38.310 --> 01:04:43.110
about being surveilled yourself,
either overtly or covertly,

01:04:43.110 --> 01:04:46.170
at different times during your work?

01:04:46.170 --> 01:04:49.140
- It reminds me of a
statement of Robert Mueller

01:04:49.140 --> 01:04:50.943
when he was the FBI director.

01:04:51.885 --> 01:04:53.993
I'm most concerned about
what we're not hearing.

01:04:57.360 --> 01:04:58.203
No stories.

01:05:00.540 --> 01:05:04.203
If it's happening,
they're good at it, yeah.

01:05:07.435 --> 01:05:09.323
- [Anna] I think there was
another question on the back.

01:05:13.880 --> 01:05:16.230
- [Participant] In the
context of talking about the,

01:05:16.230 --> 01:05:18.060
our actual security date.

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:21.700
If 9/11 hadn't happened,
would the, I threw that in.

01:05:26.422 --> 01:05:29.373
- I don't have an expert
answer to that question.

01:05:31.433 --> 01:05:34.620
Yeah, here's what I will say

01:05:34.620 --> 01:05:37.023
that it's not a direct
answer to your question.

01:05:37.950 --> 01:05:39.600
That the reason why

01:05:39.600 --> 01:05:42.360
the passage of new laws
after 9/11 was so swift

01:05:42.360 --> 01:05:45.480
is that much of those laws
had been written before 9/11,

01:05:45.480 --> 01:05:47.250
and the laws that will be passed

01:05:47.250 --> 01:05:48.540
after the next terrorist attack

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:50.090
have also already been written.

01:05:52.752 --> 01:05:54.952
- [Anna] There was a
question in the corner.

01:06:03.062 --> 01:06:04.710
- [Participant] You know, you
paint a pretty bleak picture

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:05.860
about what's happening,

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:09.540
and the ways that this
whole national security law

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:12.480
is corroding a lot of our principles.

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:14.850
And I wonder if you could
just say a little more

01:06:14.850 --> 01:06:19.736
about the FISA Court itself,
which is an Article III court

01:06:19.736 --> 01:06:21.990
and therefore should perform

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:24.240
to certain constitutional standards

01:06:24.240 --> 01:06:27.540
and how we have accountability
for the judiciary,

01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:29.520
which is normally through public access

01:06:29.520 --> 01:06:31.260
and public oversight.

01:06:31.260 --> 01:06:36.030
And is this another example
of national security concerns

01:06:36.030 --> 01:06:38.190
eroding our institutions

01:06:38.190 --> 01:06:40.593
by undermining how our
Article III courts work?

01:06:41.550 --> 01:06:44.370
- Yeah, I mean, if we
start seeing more courts

01:06:44.370 --> 01:06:48.296
adopt the same kind of
secrecy practices of the FISC,

01:06:48.296 --> 01:06:49.356
then for sure.

01:06:49.356 --> 01:06:50.670
Look, I mean, what I said before,

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:54.510
I don't object to the existence of a court

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:56.850
that issues warrants in secret.

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:59.587
Even in public Article III courts,

01:06:59.587 --> 01:07:01.080
warrants are issued in secret, right?

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:04.710
You don't send a note to someone saying,

01:07:04.710 --> 01:07:07.257
we're authorizing a wiretap against you.

01:07:07.257 --> 01:07:08.250
You go at an ex parte proceeding,

01:07:08.250 --> 01:07:10.710
and a judge gives you
a warrant, that's fine.

01:07:10.710 --> 01:07:13.593
The problem with the
FISC is mission creep.

01:07:14.490 --> 01:07:17.460
That, I mean, if all the FISC were doing

01:07:17.460 --> 01:07:19.740
were granting individualized warrants

01:07:19.740 --> 01:07:23.670
at whatever the rate of acceptance, 99.9%,

01:07:23.670 --> 01:07:26.070
that would at least be
performing, you know,

01:07:26.070 --> 01:07:28.650
a function where we
don't traditionally rely

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:31.710
on an adversarial process
or even a public process.

01:07:31.710 --> 01:07:34.770
Although with warrants, we
do expect that at some point

01:07:34.770 --> 01:07:37.050
they can be made public after
the investigation is over.

01:07:37.050 --> 01:07:39.870
And, you know, we've
been fighting for years

01:07:39.870 --> 01:07:42.240
to get the FISC to do the same thing.

01:07:42.240 --> 01:07:46.230
The problem is when you
have these broad authorities

01:07:46.230 --> 01:07:47.820
that authorize the court

01:07:47.820 --> 01:07:51.240
to issue broad programmatic warrants

01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:53.880
that implicate constitutional rights,

01:07:53.880 --> 01:07:56.220
and there's no one in the room

01:07:56.220 --> 01:07:58.350
other than the Department
of Justice and the judge

01:07:58.350 --> 01:08:00.307
to say, wait a minute.

01:08:00.307 --> 01:08:02.010
And so again, one of
the post-Snowden reforms

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:04.740
is to give the court at
least the opportunity

01:08:04.740 --> 01:08:07.830
to have an expert in the room
who is not the government.

01:08:07.830 --> 01:08:10.330
But I think, you know, we
need to continue to push

01:08:11.190 --> 01:08:14.640
for the release of their
historical opinions,

01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:17.040
on an ongoing basis for
the reason you identify.

01:08:21.660 --> 01:08:23.880
I mean, this is another problem
with classification, right.

01:08:23.880 --> 01:08:27.450
Even for that absolutely
minute category of information

01:08:27.450 --> 01:08:29.760
that is properly classified
in the first instance,

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:31.380
almost none of it should
still be classified

01:08:31.380 --> 01:08:33.873
five years later, right.

01:08:40.074 --> 01:08:41.787
- [Participant] Yeah, first it was

01:08:41.787 --> 01:08:43.143
just a really wonderful talk,

01:08:43.143 --> 01:08:45.090
and I apologize for being so late.

01:08:45.090 --> 01:08:46.320
- The answer to your question

01:08:46.320 --> 01:08:47.920
was provided before you arrived.

01:08:49.213 --> 01:08:50.046
That's okay, yeah.

01:08:50.046 --> 01:08:53.703
- [Participant] Oh, I'll pass, yeah.

01:08:54.570 --> 01:08:56.425
I'm gonna struggle a little bit

01:08:56.425 --> 01:08:57.837
to figure out how to ask this.

01:08:57.837 --> 01:08:59.058
And I have a tendency I know

01:08:59.058 --> 01:09:02.190
to ask sort of for a
prescriptive conclusion,

01:09:02.190 --> 01:09:03.690
and I'm gonna try to avoid that.

01:09:03.690 --> 01:09:06.390
But I feel like there's a way

01:09:06.390 --> 01:09:10.170
in which the story you're
telling is generalizable

01:09:10.170 --> 01:09:13.200
beyond the realm of national security.

01:09:13.200 --> 01:09:15.390
And it seems like it's a story.

01:09:15.390 --> 01:09:20.390
There's kind of a triangle
here between legal means,

01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:23.670
social movements, and in this case,

01:09:23.670 --> 01:09:26.403
important acts of civil disobedience.

01:09:27.480 --> 01:09:32.040
And as someone who's
probably has less faith

01:09:32.040 --> 01:09:33.969
in the power of law

01:09:33.969 --> 01:09:35.933
than someone who teaches
law ought to have,

01:09:36.960 --> 01:09:39.390
I guess I do have a question about

01:09:39.390 --> 01:09:41.770
whether you think there are lessons here

01:09:42.672 --> 01:09:44.313
about how change comes about,

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:49.350
and what role, what kind of relative role,

01:09:49.350 --> 01:09:52.950
those three, to the
extent we can call them

01:09:52.950 --> 01:09:56.250
three separate actors are,
the kind of, you guys,

01:09:56.250 --> 01:10:00.390
an elite legal institution
trying to bring about change,

01:10:00.390 --> 01:10:02.940
broader social movements
who seem to be motivated

01:10:02.940 --> 01:10:06.420
on the one hand by the
inculcation of fear,

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:08.970
and on the other hand, by
the inculcation of outrage

01:10:08.970 --> 01:10:12.480
when someone like Snowden can
bring it to their attention.

01:10:12.480 --> 01:10:14.850
And whether that tells us anything

01:10:14.850 --> 01:10:19.323
about how better to hold
the state accountable.

01:10:20.430 --> 01:10:24.270
- Yeah, I struggled with this question,

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:28.410
and whether I should try
to come up with something

01:10:28.410 --> 01:10:31.170
more prescriptive and oratory
at the end and say, you know,

01:10:31.170 --> 01:10:33.990
other than just let's change our values.

01:10:33.990 --> 01:10:36.510
And it's difficult,

01:10:36.510 --> 01:10:39.030
and especially for one of the
reasons that Hope suggested,

01:10:39.030 --> 01:10:41.580
which is that for a lot of
what we're talking about

01:10:42.420 --> 01:10:47.420
the victims are invisible and abroad,

01:10:47.550 --> 01:10:49.920
and so they're not going to be victims

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:52.110
around which we're going
to mobilize a movement.

01:10:52.110 --> 01:10:55.650
The exception to that might
be when we're confronted

01:10:55.650 --> 01:11:00.570
with horrific images of
sexual abuse, and, you know,

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:02.970
beatings and death at Abu Ghraib,

01:11:02.970 --> 01:11:06.090
that can actually create some
kind of accountability moment.

01:11:06.090 --> 01:11:07.950
But again, there you have it again,

01:11:07.950 --> 01:11:10.500
there's someone inside the
system breaking the law

01:11:10.500 --> 01:11:12.600
in order to put that in our faces

01:11:12.600 --> 01:11:15.213
and to force that kind of response.

01:11:16.860 --> 01:11:20.440
I think there are things
about the security context

01:11:22.110 --> 01:11:24.810
where the politics are a
little bit unique, you know,

01:11:24.810 --> 01:11:25.833
as I described,

01:11:26.792 --> 01:11:31.792
where there's just no cost
to saying we need, I mean,

01:11:32.010 --> 01:11:35.370
has there ever been in
any democracy, you know,

01:11:35.370 --> 01:11:39.540
a good example of a public
punishing political leaders

01:11:39.540 --> 01:11:42.510
for threatmongering, for fearmongering?

01:11:43.920 --> 01:11:46.140
Maybe there are, you
know, as I said yesterday,

01:11:46.140 --> 01:11:48.780
I haven't spent a lot of
time studying these phenomena

01:11:48.780 --> 01:11:50.980
more being, you know,
kicked around by them.

01:11:51.930 --> 01:11:55.020
But that would be worth looking at.

01:11:55.020 --> 01:11:57.164
And I think in part, you know,

01:11:57.164 --> 01:12:01.650
in the same way that the
government is opportunistic,

01:12:01.650 --> 01:12:05.160
and is waiting for these, you
know, moments of disruption

01:12:05.160 --> 01:12:07.803
to push additional authorities,

01:12:09.210 --> 01:12:12.091
we probably should be
doing the same thing,

01:12:12.091 --> 01:12:14.264
and know that we're going
to have these windows

01:12:14.264 --> 01:12:17.520
that are created by, you
know, big media events.

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:19.650
I think you actually
we'll see that pattern.

01:12:19.650 --> 01:12:22.320
That our big, kind of
high profile lawsuits

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:25.440
are filed six days after, you
know, the massive leak story.

01:12:25.440 --> 01:12:28.770
And so we're kind of trying
to ride that wave in some way

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:31.660
and to make sure that that person's risk

01:12:32.910 --> 01:12:36.213
is converted into some
legal or social change.

01:12:37.170 --> 01:12:39.333
But I'm going to think more about that.

01:12:49.367 --> 01:12:52.110
- [Participant] So thank
you so much for the talk.

01:12:52.110 --> 01:12:55.350
I wanted to ask a question
about the relationship between

01:12:55.350 --> 01:12:58.950
the national security state
and like the military budget,

01:12:58.950 --> 01:13:01.710
and like the military
industrial complex more broadly.

01:13:01.710 --> 01:13:03.510
Because I think you made the point

01:13:03.510 --> 01:13:06.720
that rhetorically American
politics has kind of moved on

01:13:06.720 --> 01:13:10.860
from the war on terror
as a source of fixation.

01:13:10.860 --> 01:13:12.360
And one of the things that I thought of

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:15.180
is that despite that kind
of rhetorical moving on,

01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:17.430
the kind of material
basis of that, you know,

01:13:18.780 --> 01:13:21.480
that kind of rhetorical fixation,

01:13:21.480 --> 01:13:23.730
the military budget has continued to grow.

01:13:23.730 --> 01:13:25.290
And so I'm wondering the extent

01:13:25.290 --> 01:13:28.260
to which you've experienced
a kind of integration

01:13:28.260 --> 01:13:33.090
between the kind of broader
military industrial complex,

01:13:33.090 --> 01:13:36.534
if that's not a cringey term and the

01:13:36.534 --> 01:13:37.811
national security stage.
- And no it is, I mean,

01:13:37.811 --> 01:13:39.360
I really think people, I think
that the Eisenhower speech

01:13:39.360 --> 01:13:41.940
should be in more curricula.

01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:44.253
It's short and it's really powerful.

01:13:45.567 --> 01:13:46.440
Of course, he delivered that speech

01:13:46.440 --> 01:13:48.553
as he was leaving office,

01:13:48.553 --> 01:13:49.950
and if anybody could
have had the authority

01:13:49.950 --> 01:13:52.539
during eight years in office
to do something about it,

01:13:52.539 --> 01:13:55.530
it was the leader of the Allied
forces during World War II.

01:13:55.530 --> 01:13:57.180
But even he, it was like,

01:13:57.180 --> 01:14:00.033
I'll save this for my parting shot.

01:14:02.070 --> 01:14:03.660
But no, I mean, you're absolutely right.

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:07.230
And the interplay between
that budget and classification

01:14:07.230 --> 01:14:08.370
is another aspect of it.

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:11.580
There's a great book by
William Arkin and Dana Priest

01:14:11.580 --> 01:14:14.430
about the sort of secret
surveillance budgets,

01:14:14.430 --> 01:14:17.195
and even agencies that we
don't even really know about.

01:14:17.195 --> 01:14:21.123
And these things get built,
they very rarely get unbuilt.

01:14:23.040 --> 01:14:25.410
And you know, that's why I talked about

01:14:25.410 --> 01:14:27.760
institutional incentives
more than public fears

01:14:28.890 --> 01:14:31.200
are what's driving all of this.

01:14:31.200 --> 01:14:32.700
- [Anna] There was a question.

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:36.480
I'll look to you and then
I'll come back to you again.

01:14:40.658 --> 01:14:41.491
- [Participant] Hello.

01:14:41.491 --> 01:14:44.730
I was thinking a little bit
about upstream collection,

01:14:44.730 --> 01:14:47.130
and recently I heard a talk from someone

01:14:47.130 --> 01:14:48.720
who used to work at the NSA,

01:14:48.720 --> 01:14:51.930
and he said that one argument in favor

01:14:51.930 --> 01:14:56.550
of upstream collection is
that, you know, it's not great

01:14:56.550 --> 01:14:59.550
that we're collecting
information from US citizens,

01:14:59.550 --> 01:15:01.830
but we currently just
don't have the technology

01:15:01.830 --> 01:15:05.040
to filter out communications
that aren't about, you know,

01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:06.660
what the NSA is actually targeting.

01:15:06.660 --> 01:15:09.750
And until some magical filter
drops down from heaven,

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:11.700
this is the reality that we're left with.

01:15:11.700 --> 01:15:13.380
- You know, there's another possibility

01:15:13.380 --> 01:15:15.900
which is don't collect everything.

01:15:15.900 --> 01:15:18.900
Right, it's true that if
you collect everything,

01:15:18.900 --> 01:15:20.910
it's very hard to have a magical filter

01:15:20.910 --> 01:15:23.280
that will tell you what's a US
citizen and not a US citizen.

01:15:23.280 --> 01:15:25.620
But if you think it's a
problem that they're collecting

01:15:25.620 --> 01:15:27.450
millions of communications
from US citizens,

01:15:27.450 --> 01:15:30.990
a reasonable alternative would
be don't have that program.

01:15:30.990 --> 01:15:33.540
Right, I mean, was that a
third option on his menu?

01:15:34.680 --> 01:15:36.210
- [Participant] His
argument was that, you know,

01:15:36.210 --> 01:15:38.020
in the interim we should just allow

01:15:39.210 --> 01:15:41.820
the breaches of our constitutional rights.

01:15:41.820 --> 01:15:44.520
- Well, I mean this is what,
you know, Keith Alexander,

01:15:44.520 --> 01:15:46.290
who was the head of the NSA

01:15:46.290 --> 01:15:49.143
for during the Snowden
revelations and before,

01:15:50.010 --> 01:15:51.870
and a really brilliant bureaucrat

01:15:51.870 --> 01:15:54.690
in the non-pejorative sense
of that word, you know,

01:15:54.690 --> 01:15:57.273
his mantra was collect it all,

01:15:58.170 --> 01:16:01.800
and the authorities and
the uses will follow.

01:16:01.800 --> 01:16:03.750
We can't even know if it's gonna be useful

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:05.160
until we collect it all.

01:16:05.160 --> 01:16:07.500
You know, I said this is like
going into a bar and said,

01:16:07.500 --> 01:16:08.790
give me all of the drinks

01:16:08.790 --> 01:16:11.340
so I can decide which one
I want to order, right.

01:16:11.340 --> 01:16:13.230
We have to collect everything

01:16:13.230 --> 01:16:15.402
because it might actually be useful.

01:16:15.402 --> 01:16:16.807
And the thing is, that's true.

01:16:16.807 --> 01:16:18.448
I mean, I think like
we need to actually say

01:16:18.448 --> 01:16:20.610
that it's not helpful as a predictive tool

01:16:20.610 --> 01:16:23.100
because if you collect every
communication in the world,

01:16:23.100 --> 01:16:25.170
you've built the world's biggest haystack.

01:16:25.170 --> 01:16:27.510
But it's extremely useful
as a forensic tool,

01:16:27.510 --> 01:16:28.890
so that once something has occurred,

01:16:28.890 --> 01:16:31.176
you have a surveillance time machine,

01:16:31.176 --> 01:16:32.100
you can rewind into your database,

01:16:32.100 --> 01:16:35.010
and the dots are always going
to connect in hindsight.

01:16:35.010 --> 01:16:37.500
And that's why after every
kind of terrorist attack,

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:40.470
they say, if only we've
been allowed to search

01:16:40.470 --> 01:16:42.240
all this information that we had,

01:16:42.240 --> 01:16:44.163
we would've stopped that attack.

01:16:44.163 --> 01:16:44.996
So you need to take down the wall

01:16:44.996 --> 01:16:46.050
that prevents us from looking at that.

01:16:46.050 --> 01:16:49.140
And I guarantee you this
is what would happen now.

01:16:49.140 --> 01:16:51.270
Because you know, there
are some restrictions

01:16:51.270 --> 01:16:54.840
on their ability to search
everything that's collected.

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:58.170
Although they do another
clever move, which is they say,

01:16:58.170 --> 01:17:01.410
we're not targeting US citizens,

01:17:01.410 --> 01:17:03.090
we're targeting non-citizens.

01:17:03.090 --> 01:17:05.820
Okay, regrettably, we get
millions of communications

01:17:05.820 --> 01:17:07.320
from US citizens.

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:09.990
Now we should be allowed to
search those communications

01:17:09.990 --> 01:17:12.228
because they were lawfully collected,

01:17:12.228 --> 01:17:13.560
because we weren't targeting
US citizens, right.

01:17:13.560 --> 01:17:16.050
They call that the backdoor search.

01:17:16.050 --> 01:17:18.720
And yeah, I mean, why
wouldn't they want to?

01:17:18.720 --> 01:17:21.060
I mean, if you are in a security agency,

01:17:21.060 --> 01:17:23.760
and it's your job to prevent
things from happening,

01:17:23.760 --> 01:17:25.766
to solve bad things, if they happen,

01:17:25.766 --> 01:17:27.300
of course they're gonna
want all of those things.

01:17:27.300 --> 01:17:29.580
And I don't criticize this
person for wanting it.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:32.153
It's someone else's job to
tell him he can't have it.

01:17:34.080 --> 01:17:36.510
- [Anna] Can you pass the mic this way,

01:17:36.510 --> 01:17:38.210
and you'll have the last question.

01:17:43.590 --> 01:17:44.970
- We end where we began.

01:17:44.970 --> 01:17:47.520
- [Participant] Thanks a million, Ben.

01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:51.360
So this is more just a sort
of historical perspective

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:53.700
on your argument.

01:17:53.700 --> 01:17:56.640
I mean, maybe for the
book more than for now,

01:17:56.640 --> 01:17:58.570
but I mean it seems to me

01:17:59.550 --> 01:18:03.840
how much 9/11 was really a turning point

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:07.740
is somewhat debatable in that, you know,

01:18:07.740 --> 01:18:10.710
we've seen over many decades,

01:18:10.710 --> 01:18:14.250
a drift of authority
over national security

01:18:14.250 --> 01:18:17.130
from the other branches to the executive,

01:18:17.130 --> 01:18:20.610
and a concentration of
authority within the executive.

01:18:20.610 --> 01:18:22.197
And you know,

01:18:22.197 --> 01:18:23.820
this really starts in
the Truman administration

01:18:23.820 --> 01:18:26.970
where Congress, with the
blessing of the court,

01:18:26.970 --> 01:18:29.340
gives up a lot of authority

01:18:29.340 --> 01:18:32.790
over national security
to the executive branch.

01:18:32.790 --> 01:18:37.110
And you know, also I
think, I mean I think this,

01:18:37.110 --> 01:18:40.680
the 9/11 maybe was much more
of a scaling up of things

01:18:40.680 --> 01:18:43.200
that had been going on for some time.

01:18:43.200 --> 01:18:48.180
Even in the 1990s, the NSA
was already authorizing

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:53.180
a lot of surveillance and
using various legal loopholes

01:18:53.820 --> 01:18:55.860
to get it going.

01:18:55.860 --> 01:19:00.490
So I just, you know, it's
this sort of general trend

01:19:01.620 --> 01:19:04.320
towards deference to executive authority

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:06.720
that's being intrained a very long time.

01:19:06.720 --> 01:19:10.320
And it's not just sort of post-9/11.

01:19:10.320 --> 01:19:11.550
- I think that's absolutely right,

01:19:11.550 --> 01:19:14.310
and yeah, you could start with, you know,

01:19:14.310 --> 01:19:17.730
Truman signing the National Security Act

01:19:17.730 --> 01:19:20.100
on the Sacred Cow as Air Force one, right.

01:19:20.100 --> 01:19:22.410
As we could start the lectures there.

01:19:22.410 --> 01:19:25.590
I do think though that
it's a little bit more

01:19:25.590 --> 01:19:27.510
than a difference in degree and scale.

01:19:27.510 --> 01:19:29.820
So first of all, on the surveillance side,

01:19:29.820 --> 01:19:33.600
the capabilities are night
and day different in 2006,

01:19:33.600 --> 01:19:37.140
from 1995 even, and 2010 from 2006,

01:19:37.140 --> 01:19:42.140
just the ability to collect the
whole world's communications

01:19:43.050 --> 01:19:45.600
and store them at an affordable
cost, and then search them.

01:19:45.600 --> 01:19:47.340
That didn't exist before 9/11.

01:19:47.340 --> 01:19:51.180
That capacity was created after 9/11,

01:19:51.180 --> 01:19:54.750
and the decision was made
to deploy it all in secret

01:19:54.750 --> 01:19:56.853
before the public had any kind of say,

01:19:57.737 --> 01:19:58.950
and then to install
that as an architecture.

01:19:58.950 --> 01:20:02.400
And then I think the other
point is, you know, as I said,

01:20:02.400 --> 01:20:06.030
you know, continuing to
redefine national security

01:20:06.030 --> 01:20:08.670
to take on a longer menu of threats

01:20:08.670 --> 01:20:10.200
that should not be considered related

01:20:10.200 --> 01:20:13.410
to the national defense,
even like, you know,

01:20:13.410 --> 01:20:16.380
lone wolf terrorists in the United States.

01:20:16.380 --> 01:20:19.080
And so, but I think your
point is fundamentally right,

01:20:19.920 --> 01:20:22.500
which is, I mean, I know this

01:20:22.500 --> 01:20:25.860
because when I was trying
to litigate these cases

01:20:25.860 --> 01:20:28.830
in 2006, 2007, 2008,

01:20:28.830 --> 01:20:32.790
the government was citing
cases from the 1980s involving,

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:33.960
you know, surveillance

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:36.753
and the state secrets
privilege as well, so.

01:20:38.250 --> 01:20:43.250
So yes, I think if I were going to have

01:20:44.250 --> 01:20:48.210
more than two 50-minute lectures,

01:20:48.210 --> 01:20:50.910
there would be a chart
that went like that.

01:20:50.910 --> 01:20:52.200
- [Participant] Well,
there needs to be a book,

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:53.460
and you're the guy to write it.

01:20:53.460 --> 01:20:54.293
- We'll see.

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:58.950
- [Anna] Wonderful, Ben, thank you so much

01:20:58.950 --> 01:21:01.543
for this Castle lectures

01:21:01.543 --> 01:21:04.659
and thank you everyone for
attending this today's,

01:21:04.659 --> 01:21:05.850
it's really thought
provoking and fascinating,

01:21:05.850 --> 01:21:07.800
and we look forward to seeing the book.

01:21:08.730 --> 01:21:10.432
Thank you everyone.

01:21:10.432 --> 01:21:13.599
(audience applauding)

01:21:20.622 --> 01:21:23.205
(logo chiming)

